\par
A Decision-Centered Method to Evaluate Natural Hazards
\par
Decision Aids by Interdisciplinary Research Teams
\par
Gabrielle Wong-Parodi{$\ast$} and Mitchell J. Small
\par
There is a growing number of decision aids made available to the general public by those
\par
working on hazard and disaster management. When based on high-quality scientific studies
\par
across disciplines and designed to provide a high level of usability and trust, decision aids be-
\par
come more likely to improve the quality of hazard risk management and response decisions.
\par
Interdisciplinary teams have a vital role to play in this process, ensuring the scientific validity
\par
and effectiveness of a decision aid across the physical science, social science, and engineering
\par
dimensions of hazard awareness, option identification, and the decisions made by individuals
\par
and communities. Often, these aids are not evaluated before being widely distributed, which
\par
could improve their impact, due to a lack of dedicated resources and guidance on how to sys-
\par
tematically do so. In this Perspective, we present a decision-centered method for evaluating
\par
the impact of hazard decision aids on decisionmaker preferences and choice during the de-
\par
sign and development phase, drawing from the social and behavioral sciences and a value of
\par
information framework to inform the content, complexity, format, and overall evaluation of
\par
the decision aid. The first step involves quantifying the added value of the information con-
\par
tained in the decision aid. The second involves identifying the extent to which the decision
\par
aid is usable. Our method can be applied to a variety of hazards and disasters, and will allow
\par
interdisciplinary teams to more effectively evaluate the extent to which an aid can inform and
\par
improve decision making.
\par
KEY WORDS: Decision aids; disasters; hazards; interdisciplinary; team science
\par
