\par
Preparing for natural hazards has clear benefits,
\par
yet not a lot of people do it. Research suggests a com-
\par
bination of social, economic, personal, and other fac-
\par
tors influence whether a person takes recommended
\par
personal actions (Fothergill \& Peek, 2004; Lindell \&
\par
Perry, 2012; Petkova et al., 2016; Prentice-Dunn \&
\par
Rogers, 1986). In response to this, there has been an
\par
increasing number of decision aids intended to help
\par
people make informed decisions about the natural
\par
hazards they face (see, e.g., National Oceanic and
\par
Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA, USA.
