\par
1.1. Background
\par
In general, people face three categories of
\par
threats when confronted with a natural hazard. The
\par
first is the primary risk of imminent danger occurring
\par
as a result of the event itself. For example, this could
\par
be severe flooding due to heavy precipitation during
\par
a hurricane. Appropriate and effective protective
\par
action in the case of these natural hazards could
\par
be evacuation or sheltering-in-place (Litman, 2006;
\par
Thompson, Garfin, \& Silver, 2017; Waugh, 2006).
\par
These are followed by secondary risks that occur
\par
only because the primary risk occurred, such as coal
\par
ash spilling into rivers due to the flooding from a hur-
\par
ricane, increasing people's exposure to toxic metals
\par
(Carlson \& Adriano, 1993). Finally, tertiary risks are
\par
the long-term impacts of the primary risk, such as the
\par
loss of health-care access and reduced connectivity
\par
to jobs and shopping caused by a washed out bridge
\par
in the aftermath of flooding from a hurricane (Keim,
\par
2016). Mitigating these risks involves a different, al-
\par
though potentially related, set of preparation behav-
\par
iors. Effective behaviors in this case could range from
\par
small measures, such as having an emergency plan,
\par
to large measures, such as retrofitting a home or pur-
\par
chasing flood insurance (Kreibich, Thieken, Petrow,
\par
M \textasciidieresis{} uller, \& Merz, 2005; Litman, 2006; Murakami, Sik-
\par
tel, Lucido, Winchester, \& Harbord, 2015; Shreve \&
\par
Kelman, 2014; Thompson et al., 2017; Waugh, 2006).
\par
Despite the clear benefit of taking action to
\par
protect against and prepare for disasters, most
\par
people do not prepare (Donahue, Eckel, \& Wilson,
\par
2014; Petkova et al., 2016). To better understand the
\par
reasons for this, researchers have examined potential
\par
factors that influence protective actions and have
\par
developed frameworks and models, such as Lindell
\par
and Perry's well-known Protective Action Decision
\par
Model (Lindell \& Perry, 2003, 2012; Tierney, Lindell,
\par
\& Perry, 2001), to explain the choice of alternative
\par
actions (or nonaction) in response to hazards (Ajzen,
\par
1991; Lindell, Lu, \& Prater, 2005; Lindell \& Perry,
\par
2000; 2006; Prentice-Dunn \& Rogers, 1986). Overall,
\par
findings suggest that more action is associated with
\par
higher actual risk as well as higher perceived risk
\par
(Shao et al., 2017; Shao, Xian, Lin, \& Small, 2017).
\par
These effects are influenced by an individual's social
\par
support, self-efficacy, action efficacy, and responsibil-
\par
ity, as well as prior disaster experience, sociodemo-
\par
graphic characteristics, length of residence, source
\par
of information, social cues, risk awareness, and un-
\par
derstanding of the temporal and spatial dimensions
\par
of the risk, among others (Bubeck, Botzen, \& Aerts,
\par
