\par
2012; Canfield, Bruine de Bruin, \& Wong-Parodi,
\par
2016; Jungermann, Pfister, \& Fischer, 1996; Kellens,
\par
Terpstra, \& De Maeyer, 2013; Mileti et al., 2006;
\par
Thompson et al., 2017; Wong-Parodi, Fischhoff, \&
\par
Strauss, 2017, 2018; Wray, Rivers, Whitworth, \&
\par
Jupka, 2006). It is critical that these findings be inter-
\par
preted in light of the social context in which people
\par
must operate and, if given the opportunity, consider
\par
the actions they can take to protect themselves.
\par
Indeed, taking action is often costly or time inten-
\par
sive, and research has shown that most marginalized
\par
populations often have a harder time engaging in
\par
such actions (Fothergill \& Peek, 2004; Petkova et al.,
\par
2016).
\par
Given the wide range of factors that clearly
\par
influence decision making in the context of hazards,
\par
researchers have long worked to identify the best
\par
processes for effectively communicating risk (Wood
\par
et al., 2012). This likely helps explain, in part, the
\par
increase in recent years in the number of natural
\par
hazard decision aids intended to help people and
\par
communities make better-informed risk manage-
\par
ment decisions. How information about hazards
\par
is communicated matters (Tierney et al., 2001).
\par
Indeed, Lindell and Perry propose that proactive
\par
decision making comprises five stages: attention,
\par
comprehension, acceptance, retention, and action
\par
(Lindell \& Perry, 1992). Decision aids are one way
\par
to capture attention and enhance comprehension
\par
for those interdisciplinary research teams hoping
\par
to encourage acceptance, retention, and action.
\par
They provide information about risks, uncertainties,
\par
available management options, and their associated
\par
benefits such that people are better able to make
\par
decisions in accordance with personal or societal
\par
values, preferences, and resources (Stacey et al.,
\par
2017; Wong-Parodi et al., 2014). Typically, these
\par
types of aids aim to support the hazard-related
\par
decisions that people face by making their choices
\par
and possible outcomes more explicit.
\par
Many decision aids are increasingly available
\par
online or through mobile phone apps (Houston
\par
et al., 2015; National Oceanic and Atmospheric
\par
Administration, 2017; Stacey et al., 2017), with a
\par
number including content and formats designed by
\par
interdisciplinary teams of researchers. For example,
\par
Climate Central's Surging Seas Risk Finder is an on-
\par
line decision aid created by an interdisciplinary team
\par
of scientists (Wong-Parodi \& Strauss, 2014) that
\par
allows decisionmakers---from community resiliency
\par
planners to individual homeowners---to learn about
\par
their community's risk of coastal flooding due to
\par
sea level rise, its potential impacts, and protective
\par
steps that can be taken. At the community level,
\par
the decision may be whether to invest in building
\par
sea walls, while at the individual level the decision
\par
may be whether to purchase flood insurance (Wong-
\par
Parodi et al., 2014). Rigorous process-outcome
\par
evaluation (Hicks, Barclay, Simmons, \& Loughlin,
\par
2014) is needed to examine the extent of improved
\par
understanding, active participation, and satisfaction
\par
with the decision making process, as well as the
\par
subsequent protective behaviors undertaken by the
\par
users of a decision aid. However, this is typically not
\par
possible with those aids made publicly available on-
\par
line or through mobile phone apps where there is no
\par
interaction between researchers or developers and
\par
potential decisionmakers. Therefore, it is difficult
\par
to know if the aids are usable and provide valuable
\par
information to decisionmakers (Wong-Parodi \&
\par
Bruine de Bruin, 2016; Wong-Parodi et al., 2014), let
\par
alone if they are culturally appropriate and ethically
\par
designed and implemented.
\par
While evaluation is generally acknowledged as
\par
important for improving design and assessing impact
\par
(Fischhoff et al., 2011; Houston et al., 2015; Wong-
\par
Parodi et al., 2014), interdisciplinary teams face a
\par
number of barriers to evaluation of decision aids that
\par
are intended for a general audience. Barriers include,
\par
among others (perceived or actual), high resource
\par
requirements (Fischhoff et al., 2011) and lack of clear
\par
guidance on how to systemically evaluate the success
\par
of hazard-related decision aids that may require de-
\par
cisionmakers to consider the behavior and linkages
\par
between natural, economic, social, and built systems
\par
(Wang et al., 2009; Wong-Parodi et al., 2014). In-
\par
deed, in the field of public health where decision aid
\par
design is more mature, it was only in late 2016 that
\par
the National Quality Forum issued standards for de-
\par
cisions aids intended to help patients make informed
\par
health-care decisions (National Quality Forum,
\par
2016). Hence, there is a growing need for a similar
\par
systematic method of evaluation for decision aids
\par
intended to help individuals make informed hazard
\par
mitigation decisions before they are widely deployed.
\par
2. EVALUATION STEP 1: VALUE ADDED
\par
First we assess a decision aid in the broader
\par
context of its VOI, which is generally reflective of
\par
the ability of new experiments, data, surveys, or
\par
research to improve the understanding of critical
\par
processes and system behavior, and to reduce uncer-
\par
tainty in model parameters and predictions. Decision
\par
