\par
analysts carry these improvements forward to the
\par
decisions they inform, focusing on the anticipated
\par
increase in the expected value of these decisions
\par
made with, versus without, the new information such
\par
as that contained in a decision aid. Recently, broader
\par
framings of VOI have emerged, including whether:
\par
the information is accessible, trusted, and salient; it
\par
enables conflict resolution among decisionmakers
\par
with differing beliefs and objectives; and it facilitates
\par
broader social learning and capacity building (Small,
\par
G \textasciidieresis{}
\par
uvenc \c, \& DeKay, 2014).
\par
To demonstrate the potential role of a hazard de-
\par
cision aid, a structure for a network-based influence
\par
diagram with critical elements and relationships is
\par
proposed in Fig. 1. The formulation and parameter-
\par
ization of such a network requires inputs from teams
\par
of physical scientists, engineers, and social scientists.
\par
As such, the model aims to synthesize knowledge
\par
across disciplines in a way often sought (National
\par
Research Council, 2014; Sherman-Morris, Houston,
\par
\& Subedi, 2018) but difficult to achieve in practice
\par
(Irwin et al., 2018; Kaplan, Milde, \& Cowan, 2017).
\par
The network in Fig. 1 includes discrete states
\par
for the uncertain risk level of a physical hazard
\par
(Node 1) and recent outcomes of the risk event
\par
process (Node 2). Uncertainty in the risk can be
\par
reduced by scientific studies (Node 3) with imperfect
\par
accuracy (Node 4). Decisionmakers' access to,
\par
trust in, and use of the scientific study results is
\par
characterized in Node 5. The decisionmakers' risk
\par
perception (Node 6) is a weighted average of the
\par
scientific study's results and the recent risk event
\par
outcomes, with the weight given to the scientific
\par
studies determined by the level of access and trust in
\par
the studies exhibited by decisionmakers in Node 5.
\par
The level of mitigation chosen in Node 8 is assumed
\par
to increase with the risk perception in Node 6 and
\par
decrease with the risk management cost (Node 7). In
\par
Node 9, the chosen level of mitigation is compared
\par
to the hazard risk to assess whether it is appropriate
\par
or reflective of over- or underprotection. The model
\par
is implemented as a Bayesian belief network (BBN)
\par
in Netica (see http://www.norsys.com/), with the
\par
indicated prior state probabilities and means and
\par
standard deviations shown for continuous variables
\par
that range from 0 (very low) to 1 (very high).
\par
The BBN model specification includes the
\par
conditional probability tables (CPTs) for the states
\par
in each downstream node, given all combinations of
\par
states in its upstream node(s) that influence them
\par
(indicated by a directed arrow) (see Online Ap-
\par
pendix A). The CPT relationships will be informed
\par
by interdisciplinary experts, including physical sci-
\par
entists (with particular interest in Nodes 1--4), social
\par
and behavioral scientists (with particular interest in
\par
Nodes 5--8), and others (e.g., engineers who assess
\par
and improve the costs and effectiveness of risk
\par
mitigation options). This approach allows for the
\par
study and characterization of interdependencies, as
\par
well as for evaluation of the impact of information
\par
provided in a decision aid in terms of added value to
\par
the decision making process.
\par
The network in Fig. 1 is in a ``prior condition'' or
\par
``baseline condition,'' with all top node states being
\par
equally likely, and all downstream probabilities
\par
calculated by propagating these values downward
\par
though the CPTs. This prior network serves as a
\par
baseline for evaluating the effects of new information
\par
and decisions, entered by selecting the appropriate
\par
nodes and their states in the network model. The
\par
model subsequently computes the resulting posterior
\par
probabilities for all nodes in the network. While the
\par
model shown here is highly simplified and general,
\par
a recent application of the approach is demonstrated
\par
for mitigation decisions for coastal flooding and
\par
climate change in the New York City area (Small \&
\par
Xian, 2018).
\par
Table I shows an example evaluation of a
\par
decision aid that contains low or high levels of trust-
\par
worthy scientific information on protective action
\par
decision making. Here both scenarios include high-
\par
quality scientific studies, but one with studies that
\par
are deemed to have low trustworthiness (Scenario 1)
\par
and the other with ones of high trustworthiness (Sce-
\par
nario 2). The model predicts very different outcomes.
\par
With respect to Scenario 1, we see lower levels of risk
\par
perceptions and level of mitigation, leading to under-
\par
protection. With Scenario 2, we see the opposite
\par
case with higher levels of risk perceptions and level
\par
of mitigation, leading to more appropriate levels of
\par
protection. The extent to which scientific information
\par
is deemed trustworthy in various applications has
\par
been studied from a number of disciplinary perspec-
\par
tives (e.g., Lundgren \& McMakin, 2004; Okazaki,
\par
Benavent-Climent, Navarro, \& Henseler, 2015; Renn
\par
\& Levine, 1991; Sarkki et al., 2015; Slovic, 1993; Stew-
\par
art \& Lewis, 2017). The precursors and outcomes
\par
related to trust would be informed by research con-
\par
ducted by the interdisciplinary team with members
\par
of the intended audience, both in terms of the source
\par
of the information and the way it is presented.
\par
It is important to note that not all of the
\par
factors that influence effective hazard protection
\par
are required to be at optimal levels for informed
