\par
3. EVALUATION STEP 2: USABILITY
\par
If people have the time and resources to do
\par
so, informed decision making requires that people
\par
understand the risks, benefits, and uncertainties of
\par
their options well enough to support their ability
\par
to make decisions that are consistent with their
\par
values, preferences, and resources (Wong-Parodi
\par
et al., 2014). It is important to underscore that many
\par
people, especially those who are from marginalized
\par
populations, often are unable to make informed de-
\par
cisions due to lack of resources and other constraints
\par
(Fothergill \& Peek, 2004; Petkova et al., 2016). In
\par
the context of responses to hazards, we add the addi-
\par
tional requirement of helping people to understand
\par
the behavior of, and make linkages between, natural,
\par
economic, social, and built systems. Better under-
\par
standing of linkages may lead to decisions that result
\par
in fewer unintended consequences (e.g., If I am able
\par
to make the decision to not invest in flood protection
\par
and there is permanent inundation of water, how will
\par
that affect the character of my neighborhood? How
\par
will that affect property values?).
\par
Here we present four criteria of usability that are
\par
essential for informed decision making about haz-
\par
ards. A decision aid should (i) impart knowledge of
\par
decision-relevant facts, (ii) allowing people to inte-
\par
grate these facts with their values well enough to
\par
form consistent preferences, and (iii) the enhance
\par
the ability to achieve active mastery, where they can
\par
make sound inferences based on the information
\par
available. By extension of active mastery, the deci-
\par
sion aid should (iv) help people consider the link-
\par
ages within and between systems, anticipating unin-
\par
tended consequences. This evaluation can be done
\par
remotely or in-person with members of the intended
\par
audience, such as coastal renters and homeowners,
\par
who are asked to interact with the decision aid and
\par
their interactions are assessed against these four cri-
\par
teria. This will provide the interdisciplinary research
\par
team with insights as to whether more adjustments
\par
are needed to the aid to improve its design.
\par
Table II shows proposed measures for assess-
\par
ing the extent to which a decision aid meets the
\par
criteria for usability for a sea level rise decision aid
\par
(Wong-Parodi et al., 2014). The first criterion of
\par
knowledge---understanding of the facts can be eval-
\par
uated by assessing decisionmakers' understanding
\par
of the facts through tests of pattern recognition and
\par
impacts. For example, given a higher sea level, does
