options affect who will be able to implement them in
\par
their community, with potential ethical implications
\par
and other unintended consequences? This assess-
\par
ment could help researchers identify how much peo-
\par
ple know about mitigation, and how they think about
\par
it, providing insights to researchers about the content
\par
to include and how best to present it. This assessment
\par
could be supplemented with the inclusion of a mea-
\par
sure of holism such as Choi's Analysis-Holism Scale
\par
(Choi, Koo, \& Jong An Choi, 2007), which can be
\par
used to better understand differences in responses
\par
due to decisionmakers' underlying differences in
\par
their ability to think holistically among other possi-
\par
ble differences (e.g., demographics, culture, etc.).
\par
3.1. Evaluation Step 2 Example: Sea Level Rise
\par
Decision Aid
\par
An interdisciplinary research team comprised
\par
of social and natural scientists worked together to
\par
redesign and evaluate Climate Central's Surging
\par
Seas Risk Finder (Wong-Parodi \& Strauss, 2014).
\par
This decision aid provides authoritative forecasts
\par
of coastal sea level rise and its impacts (Fig. 3)
\par
in the United States (for further information, see
\par
http://sealevel.climatecentral.org/). The aid is in-
\par
tended for a range of audiences, from homeowners
\par
deciding whether to purchase flood insurance to
\par
city planners developing recovery and resiliency
\par
programs.
\par
To assess Risk Finder, we recruited 149 U.S.
\par
adult participants through Amazon's Mechani-
\par
cal Turk (Mturk), whose demographics generally
\par
reflected the typical Mturk population (Crump,
\par
McDonnell, \& Gureckis, 2013), and randomly
\par
