\par
assigned them to one of three time frames
\par
(2020/2050/2100). Participants self-reported being
\par
an average of 36.1 years old (SD= 13.0), with half
\par
being females (47.7\%), the majority identifying as
\par
white or Caucasian (79.2\%), about half with at least
\par
a bachelor's degree (48.4\%), and many with house-
\par
hold incomes of at least \$51K (42.2\%). Participants
\par
explored Risk Finder until they felt they had enough
\par
information to tell their families about coastal
\par
flooding, and then they were assessed against three
\par
criteria as described in Table II: knowledge, consis-
\par
tency, and active mastery. Knowledge was assessed
\par
through pattern recognition, land impact and people
\par
and structural impacts with open-ended questions
\par
such as: ``What happens to the flood height (ft.) as
\par
you move the slider up and down?'' Consistency was
\par
assessed through government policy/flood tolerance
\par
and willingness to move by rating their agreement
\par
(1= completely disagree, 7= completely agree) with
\par
statements such as: ``Keeping what [I] learned about
\par
coastal floods in [County] in mind . . . I would still
\par
move with my family to [County] (if I was planning
\par
on doing that already).'' Active mastery was assessed
\par
through extrapolation above 10 ft and estimation at
\par
different time horizons with questions such as: ``Now
\par
please move the sliders below to show your estimate
\par
of how likely between today and [2020/2050/2100]
\par
it is that there will be at least one 11 ft. flood in
\par
[County].''
\par
We found that participants were able to recog-
\par
nize patterns (e.g., higher flood heights are less likely
\par
than lower ones) and impacts (e.g., water covers
\par
more land as flood height increases), thus suggest-
\par
ing that people understood the information well
\par
enough to demonstrate their knowledge. Participants
\par
expressed consistent preferences with respect to gov-
\par
ernment policy, risk tolerance, and action (e.g., low
\par
tolerance for Risk Finder users who were less willing
\par
to live in flood-prone areas if government does not
\par
take action to mitigate risk). We also found that
\par
participants were able to extrapolate beyond a flood
\par
height of 10 ft (e.g., lower chances of a flood height
\par
greater than 10 ft) and estimated higher flood heights
\par
in the future (e.g., higher flood heights in 2100 than in
\par
2020), suggesting active mastery of the information,
\par
which included projected effects due to sea level rise.
