In future work, we plan on evaluating the extent
\par
to which participants are able to think about the
\par
linkages that occur within the domain of an issue.
\par
In the case of Risk Finder, this could involve asking
\par
participants to develop an influence diagram or set of
\par
influence statements describing the linkages between
\par
and within systems, for example, asking participants
\par
to consider the economic consequences of coastal
\par
flooding to them (e.g., lost income because they are
\par
unable to go to work, the loss of home appliances
\par
and other furniture, the nonmonetized cost of lost
\par
sentimental items, etc.) and their community (e.g.,
\par
fewer tourists, fewer people interested in moving to
\par
the community, more people wanting to leave the
\par
community). This may also involve, for example,
\par
asking participants to consider how decisions made
\par
at the community level (e.g., investment in gray in-
\par
frastructure such as a levee or seawall) may influence
\par
the decisions they make at the individual level (e.g.,
\par
whether to invest in home retrofits or to purchase
\par
flood insurance).
\par
4. CONCLUDING THOUGHTS
\par
Evaluating decision aids that intend to support
\par
the decisions people must make in the face of a
\par
hazard is important to do, but can be challenging
\par
due to a lack of committed resources for that effort.
\par
Arguably, decision aids can be made more impactful
\par
through careful impact evaluation. In this Perspective
\par
we offer a decision-centered approach for evaluating
\par
the success of hazard decision aids, drawing from
\par
the VOI perspective and the social and behavioral
\par
sciences. Our two-step decision-centered approach
\par
involves first quantifying the extent to which deci-
\par
sions can be improved through new information and
\par
whether the subsequent decision aid is usable. We
\par
believe that this approach can be applied to a variety
\par
of hazards, and will allow interdisciplinary teams
\par
to more effectively evaluate the extent to which an
\par
aid can improve individual and community decision
\par
making. Such teams are needed to ensure the scien-
\par
tific validity and decision-support effectiveness of a
