  publisher = {IEEE},
  langid = {english},
  note = {IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS---PART C: APPLICATIONS AND REVIEWS, VOL. 40, NO. 2, MARCH 2010 201
\par
Reducing the Probability of Bankruptcy Through
\par
Supply Chain Coordination
\par
Xiaoyan Xu, Yanhong Sun, and Zhongsheng Hua
\par
\textbf{\emph{Abstract}---With the increasing interdependence among supply}
\par
\textbf{chain members, bankruptcy of a supply chain member may cause}
\par
\textbf{other member firms to get into financial difficulties. This paper in-}
\par
\textbf{vestigates the methods for reducing the probability of bankruptcy}
\par
\textbf{through supply chain coordination. Based on the developed mul-}
\par
\textbf{tiagent simulation model for a simple three-echelon supply chain,}
\par
\textbf{the effects of coordination mechanisms, such as information shar-}
\par
\textbf{ing (INS) and vendor-managed inventory (VMI), on reducing the}
\par
\textbf{occurrence of bankruptcy at each stage of the supply chain are}
\par
\textbf{examined. Simulation results show that such coordination mecha-}
\par
\textbf{nisms are effective in reducing the risk of bankruptcy. However, the}
\par
\textbf{key roles of these coordination mechanisms, e.g., the manufacturer}
\par
\textbf{in VMI and the retailer in INS, may be reluctant to cooperate since}
\par
\textbf{they gain less benefit or even suffer a loss from the coordination.}
\par
\textbf{Additional cooperation incentive measures, i.e., permissible delay}
\par
\textbf{in payment for INS and inventory subsidy for VMI, are thus pro-}
\par
\textbf{posed for the implementation of these coordination mechanisms,}
\par
\textbf{and simulation results confirm their validity.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Index Terms}---Bankruptcy, cooperation incentive, coordination}
\par
\textbf{mechanism, multiagent systems, supply chain management.}
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
\textbf{C}ORPORATE bankruptcy occurs when the firm becomes
\par
insolvent with liabilities that are disproportionate to as-
\par
sets. Traditionally, financial failure is attributed to poor man-
\par
agement, autocratic leadership, and difficulties in operating suc-
\par
cessfully in the market [1]. With the increasing interdependence
\par
among supply chain members, bankruptcy of a supply chain
\par
member may cause other member firms to get into financial
\par
difficulties. In network economy, this phenomenon is termed
\par
bankruptcy diffusion or bankruptcy avalanche [2], [3].
\par
The phenomenon of bankruptcy diffusion has been observed
\par
by many researchers. Since trade credit is a common form of
\par
capital interaction among businesses, Bradley and Rubach [4]
\par
examined the relationship between trade credit and the filing for
\par
bankruptcy by 131 U.S. small businesses. They found that 31\%
\par
of the 131 entrepreneurs consider nonpayments of trade credit
\par
as the most important cause for their bankruptcy. Boissay [5]
\par
developed a theoretical model, and calibrated and simulated the
\par
model using U.S. annual data over the period 1986--2004. The
\par
results showed that when customers of a sound firm go bankrupt,
\par
this firm gets into financial difficulties with a probability that
\par
