of China under Grant 70725001, Grant 70821001, and Grant 70772025. This
\par
paper was recommended by Associate Editor B. Chaib-draa.
\par
The authors are with the School of Management, University of Science
\par
and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China (e-mail: xxy204@ustc.edu.cn;
\par
yahosun@mail.ustc.edu.cn; zshua@ustc.edu.cn).
\par
Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/TSMCC.2009.2031092
\par
ranges from 4.1\% to 12.8\%. Gatti \emph{et al. }[2] found that credit
\par
linkages among firms in a network economy are a source of
\par
bankruptcy diffusion or bankruptcy avalanche because failure
\par
of fulfilling debt commitments would lead to bankruptcy chains.
\par
A supply chain is a network of entities interacting to transform
\par
raw material into finished product for customers. Since interac-
\par
tions among supply chain members on material, information,
\par
and capital are becoming increasingly intensive, financial status
\par
of a firm in a supply chain depends not only on its own manage-
\par
ment, but also on the decisions and business behaviors of other
\par
supply chain members. Despite the fact that bankruptcy diffu-
\par
sion exists in the network economy, the problem of preventing
\par
bankruptcy diffusion has drawn little attention in the researches
\par
of supply chain management.
\par
This paper investigates the methods for reducing bankruptcy
\par
probability through supply chain coordination. Bankruptcy,
\par
which refers to an unhealthy financial status, is the result of
\par
the operations of all members in the supply chain over multiple
\par
periods. Because of the complex linkages among supply chain
\par
members and the dynamically uncertain nature of revenues, fi-
\par
nancial status of the supply chain members is difficult to be
\par
investigated by any analytical supply chain model. Similarly,
\par
empirical questionnaire review or case study can hardly relate
\par
financial performance of a supply chain member with a specific
\par
operation or decision made by a supply chain member. Based
\par
on these considerations, this paper investigates financial status
\par
of the supply chain members through agent-based simulation
\par
experiments. Agent-based simulation is an effective approach
\par
for its encapsulation of the complex internal behaviors of the
\par
members [6], and thus, has been extensively used in the field of
\par
supply chain modeling (e.g., [7]--[9]). The agent-based simula-
\par
tion approach facilitates us to investigate the effects of various
\par
supply chain coordination mechanisms by modifying the behav-
\par
iors of the agents in the simulation model. Consequently, corpo-
\par
rate bankruptcy can be easily related to operations or decisions
\par
made according to a supply chain coordination mechanism in
\par
the simulation experiments.
\par
In supply chain management, many coordination mechanisms
\par
have been proposed to improve the operational performance of a
\par
supply chain, e.g., information sharing (INS), vendor-managed
\par
inventory (VMI), and various cooperation incentive schemas.
\par
However, the problem whether these coordination mechanisms
\par
can effectively reduce the risk of bankruptcy in a supply chain
\par
has not been considered before in the scientific literature. For
\par
a three-echelon serial supply chain that consists of a manu-
\par
facturer, a distributor, and a retailer, this paper examines the
\par
effectiveness of these coordination mechanisms in reducing the
\par
risk of bankruptcy at each stage of the supply chain. Results
\par
1094-6977/\$26.00 \copyright{} 2009 IEEE
\par
Authorized licensed use limited to: University of Science and Technology of China. Downloaded on March 15,2010 at 20:40:56 EDT from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.202 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS---PART C: APPLICATIONS AND REVIEWS, VOL. 40, NO. 2, MARCH 2010
\par
of the agent-based simulation suggest that INS and VMI can
\par
effectively reduce the risk of bankruptcy in the supply chain.
\par
However, the key roles of the coordination mechanisms, e.g.,
\par
the manufacturer in VMI and the retailer in INS, gain less ben-
\par
efit or even suffer a loss from the supply chain coordination.
\par
Additional cooperation incentive measures, i.e., permissible de-
\par
lay in payment for INS and inventory subsidy for VMI, are thus
\par
applied to solve the problem. Simulation results verify the valid-
\par
ity of these cooperation incentive measures and show that these
\par
incentive measures can further decrease the risk of bankruptcy.
\par
The main contributions of this paper are threefolds. First, an
\par
agent-based simulation model is built to investigate the relation-
\par
ship between the operational coordination mechanisms and the
\par
financial status of firms in the supply chain. Second, it is found
\par
that supply chain coordination is effective in reducing the risk
\par
of bankruptcy. Third, by observing the financial status of the
\par
supply chain members, it is found that INS and VMI are not
\par
costless, and some cooperation incentive measures are neces-
\par
sary for the implementation of such coordination mechanisms,
\par
which can further lessen the risk of bankruptcy in the supply
\par
chain.
\par
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In
\par
Section II, we review the literatures related to bankruptcy dif-
\par
fusion and supply chain coordination mechanisms. The tradi-
\par
tionally managed supply chain (TSC) model and coordination
\par
mechanisms are described in Section III. The equity-updating
\par
process for firms in the supply chain model is elaborated in
\par
Section IV. Simulation analyses on the coordination mecha-
\par
nisms and the additional incentive measures are presented in
\par
Sections V and VI, respectively. The paper ends with some
\par
conclusion remarks.
\par
II. LITERATURE REVIEW
\par
The term bankruptcy diffusion is not new. In financial man-
\par
agement and economics, many researchers have observed and
\par
investigated the phenomena of financial contagion in financial
\par
systems and bankruptcy diffusion in network economy. The next
\par
two paragraphs successively present the point of view of these
\par
areas.
\par
The phenomena of financial contagion were first observed in
\par
banking system. By modeling financial contagion as an equi-
\par
librium phenomenon, Allen and Gale [10] analyzed how bank
\par
crisis spreads by contagion and put forward a theory of ``finan-
\par
cial contagion'' in a network model of the interbank market.
\par
They suggested the complete structure of interregional claims
\par
for preventing crisis diffusion. Rijckeghem and Weder [11] pro-
\par
vided empirical evidence in support of the view that spillovers
\par
through common bank lenders were important in transmitting
\par
currency crises. Using a model with banking and insurance sec-
\par
tors, Allen and Carletti [12] argued that credit risk transfer can
\par
also lead to financial contagion between the two sectors and in-
\par
crease the risk of crises. These researches focused on the causes
\par
of financial contagion among financial institutions.
\par
The research on financial contagion has also been extended
\par
from banking system to general interfirm situation. Based on
\par
the background of the slump in Japan in the 1990s, Kiyotaki
\par
and Moore [13] found that contagion is diffused through bal-
\par
ance sheet effects, i.e., when a firm goes bankrupt, it cannot pay
\par
the debt to its creditors, which may lead its creditor to finan-
\par
cial difficulties. Bradley and Rubach [4] examined the relation-
\par
ship between trade credit and the filing for bankruptcy by 131
\par
U.S. small businesses. Gatti \emph{et al. }[2] modeled a network econ-
\par
omy with three sectors: downstream firms, upstream firms, and
\par
banks. Through agent-based simulations, they found that credit
\par
interlinkages among agents are a source of bankruptcy diffu-
\par
sion because failure of fulfilling debt commitments would lead
\par
to bankruptcy chains. Boissay [5] also emphasized the role of
\par
trade credit by developing a theoretical model that bankruptcy
\par
propagates through a network of firms linked by trade credit
\par
and simulating the model with U.S. annual data to quantify
\par
the probability of diffusion. By reviewing 1695 bankruptcy
\par
filings between 1978 and 2004 from Bankruptcy DataSource
\par
Index, Hertzel \emph{et al. }[14] investigated how financial distress
\par
and bankruptcy affect a filing firm's customers and suppliers,
\par
and found that distress related to bankruptcy filings is associ-
\par
ated with negative and significant stock price effects for suppli-
\par
ers. These researches mainly focused on the indirect interaction
\par
among firms, which takes place through trade credit, bank loans,
\par
and the endogenous determination of the interest rate.
\par
Direct interactions among firms were first investigated by
\par
Battiston \emph{et al. }[3] in the generation of bankruptcy avalanches.
\par
They [3] presented a simple model of a production network
\par
in which firms are linked by supplier--customer relationships
\par
involving extension of trade credit. Through simulation analy-
\par
ses, they identified roles of some factors in bankruptcy diffusion,
\par
e.g., trade credit, supply failure, and the structure of the network
\par
of interactions. However, they did not provide any operational
\par
solution for preventing the propagation of bankruptcies.
\par
Supply chain is a typical representative of network economy
\par
that has been intensively investigated from the standpoint of
\par
operations management. Researches in supply chain manage-
\par
ment have developed numerous types of operational coordina-
\par
tion mechanisms, e.g., INS and VMI, to improve the perfor-
\par
mance of firms in a supply chain. Using analytical models, Lee
\par
\emph{et al. }[15] evaluated the benefits of demand INS between re-
\par
tailers and their upstream suppliers, and found that the value of
\par
demand INS can be quite high, especially when demands are
\par
significantly correlated over time. Based on a two-stage supply
\par
chain, Tsung [16] quantified and compared the impact of dif-
\par
ferent INS strategies on process and product quality, and found
\par
that real-time INS may lead to dramatic quality improvement
\par
for an assembly process. Moyaux \emph{et al. }[8] showed that INS
\par
as a coordination mechanism is effective in reducing the bull-
\par
whip effect in a supply chain. Because INS usually ``provides
\par
ignorable levels of benefits'' for retailers, Sari [17] suggested
\par
that upstream members (e.g., suppliers or manufacturers) offer
\par
incentives (e.g., VMI or collaborative planning, forecasting, and
\par
replenishment) to retailers in return for INS.
\par
VMI, also known as continuous replenishment or supplier-
\par
managed inventory, is one of the most widely discussed part-
\par
nering initiatives for encouraging collaboration and INS among
\par
supply chain members [18]. Under VMI, the retailer determines
\par
its sales quantity but delegates the entire replenishment strategy
\par
Authorized licensed use limited to: University of Science and Technology of China. Downloaded on March 15,2010 at 20:40:56 EDT from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.XU \emph{et al.}: REDUCING THE PROBABILITY OF BANKRUPTCY THROUGH SUPPLY CHAIN COORDINATION 203
\par
Fig. 1. Flow of goods and information under TSC.
\par
to the supplier, and correspondingly, transfers all of the carrying
\par
costs to the supplier [19]. Since VMI permits the chain to exploit
\par
economies of scale in the production and distribution process,
\par
and by centralizing the replenishment process, VMI can offer
\par
a competitive advantage for the retailer. Effectiveness of other
\par
supply chain coordination mechanisms, e.g., permissible delay
\par
in payment [20] and inventory subsidy [21], can also be found
\par
easily in the supply chain literature.
\par
Many theoretical and practical evidences have proven the ef-
\par
fectiveness of these coordination mechanisms in improving sup-
\par
ply chain performance. However, we still know little about the
\par
effectiveness of these coordination mechanisms in preventing
\par
the risk of bankruptcy diffusion in a supply chain, and there is a
\par
gap between supply chain operations and financial status in the
\par
research of supply chain management, which is the aspiration
\par
for the present paper.
\par
III. DESCRIPTION OF THE SUPPLY CHAIN
\par
This paper investigates a typical three-echelon supply chain
\par
that consists of a retailer, a distributor, and a manufacturer. The
\par
supply chain is built to meet random market demand of a single
\par
product, Dt, in discrete time periods t(t = 1,2,...). In each
\par
period, the retailer orders certain amount of the product from
\par
the distributor to meet market demand of the product, and the
\par
manufacturer orders materials from the outside supplier to fulfill
\par
the orders of the distributor. Since we assume that the supply
\par
chain members replenish their inventories once every period,
\par
to monitor the inventory of the product closely, the length of a
\par
period is assumed to be a week or a few days. In this section,
\par
we first describe the operation process of the supply chain under
\par
traditional arrangement, and then, briefly introduce two typical
\par
coordination mechanisms, i.e., INS and VMI.
\par
\emph{A. Traditionally Managed Supply Chain}
\par
In the traditional arrangement, demand information flows up-
\par
stream, beginning with the market. The information flowing
\par
between the echelons is not identical. The retailer receives mar-
\par
ket demand information; however, the distributor receives the
\par
retailer's order information, not market demand information.
\par
Likewise, the manufacturer receives the distributor's order in-
\par
formation. Fig. 1 illustrates how goods and information flow
\par
between members in the supply chain. The supply chain under
\par
this arrangement is termed the TSC [17].
\par
The main operational decision-making problem for each
\par
member under TSC is to decide the amount of goods ordered
\par
