\par
Since a supply chain is composed of several independent firms,
\par
operational relationship between firms can be viewed as regu-
\par
lated by a contract with three cost terms, namely, a holding cost
\par
rate, a backorder cost rate, and a fixed ordering cost [23]. For
\par
each upstream member in the supply chain, if it fails to fulfill
\par
the orders placed by its downstream member firm in the given
\par
procurement lead time, it has to pay its downstream member the
\par
shortage penalty cost according to the contract. Thus, shortage
\par
costs at the manufacturer and the distributor can be assumed
\par
to be real payment. We assume linear cost function of shortage
\par
penalty with rate of bi. We also assume linear cost function
\par
of inventory holding with rate of hi in the event of overstock.
\par
The sum of inventory holding cost and shortage penalty cost is
\par
usually called operating cost [15].
\par
Under TSC, each member of the supply chain makes ordering
\par
decision that minimizes its own operating costs without consid-
\par
ering the impact of this decision on the performance of other
\par
members. When the fixed ordering cost is ignored, it is well
\par
recognized that member i(i = 1,2,3) can minimize its oper-
\par
