VI. COOPERATION INCENTIVE MEASURES FOR INS AND VMI
\par
The previous section has shown that INS and VMI can effec-
\par
tively reduce the average number of occurrences of bankruptcy
\par
in the supply chain; however, the key roles of these coordination
\par
mechanisms may be less motivated to participate in such coop-
\par
eration because they gain less benefit or even suffer a loss from
\par
the supply chain coordination. To motivate these supply chain
\par
members, we propose two cooperation incentive measures, i.e.,
\par
conditional delay in payment for INS (CINS) and inventory
\par
subsidy for VMI (IVMI).
\par
In some experiments, it is observed that the number of occur-
\par
rences of bankruptcy at the retailer is increased under INS com-
\par
pared with that under TSC. The reduced benefit of the retailer
\par
in these experiments can be interpreted as a new context of the
\par
``cost of INS,'' which is different from those costs of INS that are
\par
recognized in the previous researches, e.g., cost of investment
\par
in IT and cost of leaking confidential competitive data [37].
\par
To further investigate how the ``new'' cost of INS is incurred
\par
under INS, we present detailed analyses on the costs of the
\par
retailer given the parameters of the supply chain set in experi-
\par
ment \#13. Fig. 3 illustrates the average operating and financial
\par
costs of the retailer under TSC and INS over periods [1, 5000].
\par
In Fig. 3, i(i = 1,2,... ,10) on x-axis denotes time periods
