\emph{A. Conditional Delay in Payment for INS}
\par
Permissible delay in payment is a common form of trade
\par
credit, which permits the delay of payment after the buyer's
\par
receipt of goods [20]. As a common practice in industries, this
\par
mechanism has been shown to be effective in alleviating the
\par
temporary pressure of capital turnover of the downstream mem-
\par
bers and then stimulating their demand for goods [20]. Under
\par
INS, if a delay of certain periods is permitted in payment by
\par
its upstream member when a member falls into the state of
\par
low liquidity, then the financial cost can be avoided and the
\par
performance of the downstream member will be improved. To
\par
motivate the retailer under INS, we propose a CINS.
\par
