\emph{B. Inventory Subsidy for VMI}
\par
Inventory subsidy is a common measure of allocating operat-
\par
ing cost among firms [21], which has various operation modes
\par
in VMI systems. Gerchak and Wang [38] investigated a revenue-
\par
plus-surplus subsidy scheme in which the upstream supplier is
\par
partially paid by the downstream assembler for the delivered
\par
components that are not sold. Nagarajan and Rajagopalan [39]
\par
considered a holding inventory subsidy contract in which the
\par
holding cost incurred at the retailer is allocated between the
\par
manufacturer and the retailer. To motivate the manufacturer un-
\par
der VMI, we adopt the operation mode that the downstream
\par
members pay an inventory subsidy to the manufacturer.
\par
Following [39], we assume that the inventory subsidy paid by
\par
the retailer is a proportion {$\eta$} (0 {$\leq$} {$\eta$} {$\leq$} 1) of the inventory hold-
\par
ing cost incurred at the retailer, and the inventory subsidy paid
\par
by the distributor is a proportion (1- {$\eta$})/2 of the sum of the in-
\par
ventory holding costs incurred at the retailer and the distributor.
