\par
VII. CONCLUSION
\par
In this paper, we have developed a general modeling frame-
\par
work for supply chains to explore the effects of operational
\par
interactions among firms on their financial status, which pro-
\par
vides the possibility of connecting the occurrence of bankruptcy
\par
to a specific supply chain coordination mechanism. This study
\par
makes a significant contribution to the supply chain management
\par
and financial management literature by systematically examin-
\par
ing the effectiveness of supply chain coordination mechanisms
\par
Authorized licensed use limited to: University of Science and Technology of China. Downloaded on March 15,2010 at 20:40:56 EDT from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.214 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS---PART C: APPLICATIONS AND REVIEWS, VOL. 40, NO. 2, MARCH 2010
\par
in reducing the probability of bankruptcy in a supply chain con-
\par
text. Overall, the results show that INS and VMI can effectively
\par
reduce the probability of bankruptcy of members in the supply
\par
chain, and these coordination mechanisms usually need addi-
\par
tional cooperation incentive measures such as those we propose
\par
because the key roles of the coordination mechanisms may gain
\par
less benefit or even suffer a loss from the supply chain coordi-
\par
nation.
\par
This paper investigates the effectiveness of supply chain coor-
\par
dination in reducing the probability of bankruptcy in the supply
\par
chain with a relatively simple structure. The obtained conclu-
\par
sion can be directly extended to the multiechelon supply chain
\par
with more than one firm in each stage if there is no horizon-
\par
tal competition among these firms. If there exists horizontal
\par
competition or other types of complex relationship between
\par
firms in the supply chain, the conclusions in this paper need
\par
to be respeculated, which will be the topic of future research.
\par
In these researches, the behavior of member firms will be de-
\par
scribed by revising the supply chain model given in Section III
\par
and the simulation model given in Section V accordingly. We
\par
provide a method for reducing the risk of bankruptcy without
\par
directly considering the bankruptcy diffusion mechanism in the
\par
supply chain. It is interesting to analytically relate bankruptcy
\par
diffusion mechanism with its preventing or mitigating method
\par
in future research. In addition, different supply chain scenar-
\par
ios characterized by different supply chain parameters, e.g., the
\par
occurrence of emergency events and different replenishment
\par
lead times and market demand process, also deserve further
\par
investigation.
