Singapore
\par
\& Many practical applications in the medical domain require a cooperative decision with
\par
multiple entities (agents). These applications are instances of a multi-agent decision problem. This
\par
complex decision problem often concerns a large knowledge domain and involves some agency
\par
properties. It disables traditional methods on probabilistic graphical decision models. In this article,
\par
we propose a new representation including multiply sectioned influence diagrams (MSIDs) and
\par
hyper relevance graphs (HRGs). An MSID represents decision problems involving multiple agents
\par
in a distributed and flexible fashion, while an HRG encodes organizational relationships in a
\par
multi-agent system. Subsequently, a symbolic method is extended to facilitate the model verification
\par
with the aim of building a valid decision model. An evaluation algorithm based on the junction
\par
tree algorithm is developed to solve an MSID. Some relevant evaluation strategies are analyzed. The
\par
decision problem on the Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) control is illustrated with our
\par
proposed methodologies throughout this article.
\par
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) is a serious infectious disease
\par
that could potentially develop into an epidemic or even an endemic. Its
\par
uncertain and various sources have frustrated the medical community
\par
and policymakers. Its outbreak causes unexpected loss everywhere in
\par
the world. Beyond all doubt, to relieve this loss needs a collaborative
\par
effort of multiple nations concerning their own local as well as global
\par
benefits.
\par
The decision problem on SARS involves many nations or communities,
\par
even those without the outbreak of SARS. These involved nations are
\par
collectively seeking good solutions to control SARS in the whole
\par
The article was partially finished when Y. Z. completed his PhD study at the National University of
\par
Singapore. The authors would like to thank Dr. Tze-Yun Leong and members of the Biomedical
\par
Decision Engineering Research Group at the National University of Singapore for their helpful
\par
comments.
\par
Address correspondence to Yifeng Zeng, Department of Computer Science, Aalborg University,
\par
Selma Lagerlo \textasciidieresis fs Vej 300, Aalborg East, DK-9220, Denmark. E-mail: yfzeng@cs.aau.dk104 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
community in order to avoid more loss. This kind of decision problem is a
\par
distributed one involving multiple agents. It is referred to as a multi-agent
\par
decision problem in this article. Some characters of this decision scenario
\par
as follows: 1) Agents are distributed geographically or physically. Each
\par
nation or community is an independent entity in the world. It is not easy
\par
and reasonable to merge them into a single object. 2) Agents are cooperat-
\par
ive. Although an agent is an independent entity, it still needs cooperation
\par
for solving a certain decision problem. The cooperation is based on public
\par
information they share. In the above decision scenario, these nations would
\par
share some valuable information such as the available SARS reports. 3)
\par
Agents' privacy is protected. Although agents are in a cooperative setting,
\par
they intend to retain their privacy. For example, the involved nations could
\par
not release all of their own information such as their hospital facilities. 4)
\par
Agents' decisions and observations are interleaved; however, their interac-
\par
tions follow a sequential order. In a distributed environment, agents need
\par
some observations from their adjacent agents to support the decision-
\par
making. For instance, in the decision scenario previously mentioned, a
\par
nation needs to be informed of the SARS report from its adjacent nation
\par
in order to adopt some actions. 5) Agent's organizational relationships
\par
exist. In a cooperative decision problem, an agent may need requisite infor-
\par
mation for its decision-making while this information could be only
\par
obtained from its adjacent agents. Hence, a certain organizational relation-
\par
ship exists among these agents. Meanwhile, this kind of organizational
\par
relationship could be described by the relation between the information
\par
and the supported decisions. 6) Agents seek their individual objectives
\par
while they expect a cooperative solution. In a distributed decision problem,
\par
every agent has its own goal since it is selfish. It wants to make the best
\par
decision on its own through the cooperation in which it could access some
\par
requisite information. For a cooperative solution globally, agents contribute
\par
by releasing the honest and up-to-date information by which they wish to
\par
help decision-making in their adjacent agents. Hence, in this kind of
\par
decision scenario, what cooperative agents are concerned with is the shared
\par
information. They are unwilling to compromise their own utility with the
\par
consideration of others' decision-making. Intuitively, a multi-agent decision
\par
problem always relates to a large knowledge domain since it concerns a
\par
complex decision problem involving multiple agents. The aim of this
\par
article is to represent and solve this kind of decision problem. The poten-
\par
tial approach for solving multi-agent decision problems should extend
\par
traditional graphical decision models as well as exploit characteristics of
\par
multiple agents.
\par
The aforementioned decision scenario could not be solved using
\par
traditional methods on probabilistic graphical decision models such as
\par
influence diagrams (Howard and Matheson 1984). These mature modelMulti-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 105
\par
representations were originally developed to solve decision problems for
\par
the single agent paradigm. Much formalism has also been proposed to cope
\par
with some special decision problems, such as the dynamic influence dia-
\par
grams (Tatman and Shachter 1990), the time-critical dynamic influence
\par
diagrams (Xiang and Poh 1999), the unconstrained influence diagrams
\par
(Jensen and Vomlelova 2002), and the sequential influence diagrams
\par
(Jensen, Nielsen, and Shenoy 2004). The dynamic influence diagrams and
\par
time-critical dynamic influence diagrams emphasize the solving of decision
\par
problems encoding time series through concise representations. The uncon-
\par
strained influence diagrams and sequential influence diagrams try to solve
\par
asymmetric decision problems by relaxing usual constraints of influence
\par
diagrams. However, this work was not aimed at the multi-agent decision prob-
\par
lem until the framework of the multi-agent influence diagrams (MAID)
\par
(Koller and Milch 2001) was initiated to represent and solve a game prob-
\par
lem. Extending from a single agent-based influence diagram, the MAID still
\par
lacks the ability to deal with a large knowledge domain and overlooks some
\par
agency features. Furthermore, the situation we intend to address involves
\par
decision problems for every interacted agent which has obtained beneficial
\par
information from its adjacent agents in a distributed and unpredictable
\par
environment. This situation is quite different from games; hence, many
\par
methodologies in decision theory could be utilized. Recently, some pro-
\par
gresses (Xiang 2002; Gmytrasiewicz 2003; Portinale, Magro, and Torasso
\par
2004) have been made on the multi-agent reasoning system. They focus
\par
on reasoning and planning involving multiple agents; however, they do
\par
not consider agents' decision-making. Hence, a piece of new work is
\par
required to address the multi-agent decision problem.
\par
In this article, we propose a new framework of multiply sectioned
\par
influence diagrams (MSIDs) and hyper relevance graphs (HRGs) to solve
\par
a multi-agent decision problem. An MSID encodes agency features and
\par
represents a large and complex decision problem with the divide and con-
\par
quer strategy. An HRG, on the other hand, spells out various organizational
\par
relationships that bind multiple agents in an organization evolving over
\par
time. They are interrelated to provide a reliable and compact framework.
\par
Accordingly, the MSID and HRG together are a reusable and coherent
\par
framework that can be utilized to address a general decision problem.
\par
Methods for the verification of models built on MSID and HRG are
\par
discussed and illustrated in this article. The symbolic verification method
\par
extending our past work (Zeng and Poh 2003) is proposed. Furthermore,
\par
an indirect method to evaluate an MSID is formulated when an MSID is
\par
transformed into a multiple rooted cluster tree. The relevant evaluation
\par
strategies are also analyzed.
\par
This article is organized as follows. Firstly, we propose the represen-
\par
tation of MSID and HRG, Then, the issue of model verification on MSID106 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
and HRG is illustrated. After that, we discuss an indirect method to evaluate
\par
an MSID. Finally, we conclude the article with some possible applications of
\par
our proposed methodologies and some remarks about future work.
\par
GRAPHICAL REPRESENTATION
\par
In this section, a new representation will be proposed to address the
\par
multi-agent decision problem. As a prelude to this major discussion, a con-
\par
cise review on some basic concepts of graphical decision models is provided
\par
in the preceding parts of this section.
\par
Basic Concepts
\par
Influence diagram (Howard and Matheson 1984) is a basic probabilistic
\par
graphical decision model for representing the single-agent decision-
\par
making in an uncertain environment. It is composed of chance nodes,
\par
decision nodes, and value nodes all of which together represent a decision
\par
scenario in a directed acyclic graph (DAG). In influence diagrams, chance
\par
nodes describe agent's observations, including both agent's knowledge on
\par
its environment and agent's beliefs on its interacting entities; decision
\par
nodes represent agents' decision alternatives and value nodes specify an
\par
agent's utility function.
\par
Recently, much of the research work (Nielsen 2001; Shachter 1998,
\par
1999) on graphical decision models is concerned with such a kind of prob-
\par
lem: what is the required information for the decision-making in influence
\par
diagrams? To answer this question, firstly, two types of chance nodes, called
\par
requisite probability node and requisite observation node, are defined
\par
(Shachter 1998, 1999). The requisite probability nodes for a particular
\par
decision are those nodes whose (conditional) probability distributions
\par
might be needed to evaluate the decision problem starting with that
\par
decision. The requisite observation nodes for a particular decision are
\par
those nodes that should be observed before the decision in order to evalu-
\par
ate the decision problem starting with that decision. Secondly, the
\par
procedure Decision Bayes-ball (Shachter 1998, 1999) was proposed to
\par
identify this requisite information in influence diagrams. The procedure
\par
Decision Bayes-ball utilizes the concept of D-separation (Pearl 1988) to
\par
determine whether Bayes-ball bounces back or passes through the way
\par
when it meets different types of nodes in different directions from
\par
which the Bayes-ball comes. Later, an outstanding piece of research work
\par
(Nielsen 2001) tries to refine the procedure Decision Bayes-ball to obtain
\par
a minimal set of information being required for a particular decision. It
\par
first specifies the relevant utility functions for a particular decision node.
\par
Then it detects required nodes for this decision node using some rulesMulti-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 107
\par
based on D-separation. We shall refer to this new procedure as refined
\par
Decision Bayes-ball. (In the succeeding context, the Decision Bayes-ball
\par
procedure will be used to denote both the basic Decision Bayes-ball
\par
procedure and the refined Decision Bayes-ball procedure.)
\par
Figure 1 shows an example of influence diagrams. The influence dia-
\par
gram represents an uncertain decision problem involving a single agent.
\par
It consists of a single decision node d, a set of chance nodes fa; b; c; eg,
\par
and a single value node v. Through the procedure Decision Bayes-ball, it
\par
is found that node e and node b are a requisite probability node and a
\par
requisite observation node for decision node d, respectively. Thus, these
\par
two nodes compose the requisite information for decision d in this decision
\par
problem.
\par
In a multi-agent decision problem, multiple agents cooperate with each
\par
other to obtain desirable decisions while they share some common infor-
\par
mation. Here, it is assumed that an agent could release the information
\par
on its observations. Accordingly, only chance nodes are shared among
\par
adjacent agents. Currently, another piece of work most relevant to our
\par
theme in this article is the representation of multiply sectioned bayesian
\par
networks (MSBNs) (Xiang 2002). In fact, an MSBN is a collection of
\par
Bayesian networks (BNs) (Pearl 1988) which are similar to influence dia-
\par
grams but without decision nodes and value nodes. An MSBN is also a
\par
DAG having only chance nodes. Each local Bayesian network in an MSBN
\par
representing a single agent's reasoning engine. All together, an MSBN pro-
\par
vides a coherent framework for probabilistic reasoning in a cooperative
\par
multi-agent distributed interpretation system.
\par
One important concept, called D-Sepnode (Xiang 2002), gives some
\par
constraints to subnets (local Bayesian networks) in an MSBN. A node
\par
contained in more than one subnet in an MSBN is a D-Sepnode if there
\par
is one subnet that contains all of its parents. In an MSBN, all pairwise
\par
intersections between subnets should be D-Sepset which is composed of
\par
FIGURE 1 An example of influence diagrams.108 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
D-Sepnodes. Through D-Sepsets, agents communicate with each other to
\par
arrive at a consistent coordination in a distributed reasoning system.
\par
Hence, D-Sepsets are also called agent interface in an MSBN.
\par
Figure 2 shows an example MSBN. The MSBN is a DAG that comprises
\par
two local BNs, namely, B0 and B1. Each BN represents an individual agent's
\par
reasoning engine. Through the set of common nodes fa; b; cg representing
\par
their public information, these two agents communicate with each other to
\par
obtain a full and consistent reasoning in the multi-agent system. In Figure 2,
\par
common nodes fa; b; cg are D-Sepnodes since all of their parents reside in
\par
one local BN. For example, parents of node b and node d are nodes fd; ag
\par
and node d, respectively. All the parents are in B1, while parents of node c
\par
are nodes fb; g g residing in B0. Hence, these common nodes form the
\par
D-Sepset between B0 and B1 in the MSBN.
\par
D-Sepsets are the only communication channels among agents in an
\par
MSBN. In this article, the D-Sepset concept is extended to represent every
\par
intersection among adjacent subnets, not just between each pair of subnets.
\par
Moreover, an irreducible D-Sepset is defined here. An irreducible D-Sepset
\par
is a set of D-Sepnodes that encode the necessary information. Due to the
\par
privacy protection matter, agents are not willing to release all of their
\par
own information so that they just share the necessary information which
\par
does benefit adjacent agents' decision-making. The necessary information
\par
may include the requisite information and the supporting information
\par
for agents' decision-making. The requisite information is encoded in either
\par
required probability nodes or required observation nodes and is required
\par
for the decision-making. The supporting information is not required for
\par
agents' decision-making; however, this information supports adjacent
\par
agents' decision-making. For example, an agent may give the complemen-
\par
tary information to its adjacent agents who cannot access this information
\par
by themselves. Thus, an irreducible D-Sepset not only provides a concise
\par
FIGURE 2 An example of MSBN.Multi-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 109
\par
representation, but also indicates the most economical information for
\par
supporting decision-making in a multi-agent decision problem.
\par
Definition of MSID
\par
The previous work on graphical decision models fails to deal with a
\par
multi-agent decision problem such as influence diagrams and their
\par
evolutionary representations. Two major reasons are stated. Firstly, those
\par
formalisms emphasize the single agent-based decision problem. Thus, they
\par
do not consider cooperation among agents and properties of multiple
\par
agents such as agents' privacy and organizational relationships. Secondly,
\par
those graphical models are not scalable. The multi-agent decision problem
\par
is a large and complex decision problem so that it is hard to concisely
\par
represent a bulk of elements and their relationships within a single model.
\par
As an extended model from Bayesian networks, an MSBN provides a
\par
coherent framework for multiple agents reasoning. However, it does not
\par
address agents' decision-making problems. This pioneering work lays out
\par
a foundation for the concept of MSID.
\par
Definition 1. An MSID I is a set of influence diagrams Ij such that each dia-
\par
gram Ij represents an agent j and the shared chance nodes among adjacent
\par
diagrams Ii ; Ij ;. . . ; Ik comprise an irreducible D-Sepset Sij...k.
\par
For instance, for agent i and agent j, the MSID is denoted by I{$\frac{1}{4}$} Ii [ Ij.
\par
The D-Sepset between influence diagram Ii and influence diagram Ij is
\par
denoted by Sij \dh i 6{$\frac{1}{4}$} j\TH. It can be seen that an MSID, defined as I{$\frac{1}{4}$} Sj Ij,
\par
involves two concepts: an agent-based influence diagram Ij and an
\par
irreducible D-Sepset Sij...k.
\par
In brief, an MSID is a set of local influence diagrams that reflect an
\par
individual agent's knowledge, actions, and preference. These agents
\par
communicate with each other through a D-Sepset that indicates public
\par
information. Except for the shared information in a D-Sepset, other infor-
\par
mation is protected in each local influence diagram. The shared
\par
information indicates agents' beliefs on an uncertain environment. It
\par
may be affected by agents' actions. The public information can also exert
\par
its influence on agents' behavior. To make a consistent representation in
\par
an MSID, Bayesian networks representing an agent, which is only an
\par
information-collecting entity without actions or behaviors, are called
\par
degenerated influence diagrams. Consequently, an MSID is a hybrid
\par
graphical decision model that is a combination of Bayesian networks and
\par
influence diagrams. In this sense, an MSBN is a special case of MSID in
\par
which all agents do not make decisions and are represented by local
\par
Bayesian networks.110 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
The definition of MSID implies that an MSID should comply with three
\par
constraints: DAG structure, D-Sepset of agent interface, and irreducible D-
\par
Sepset. The first constraint prohibits any directed cycle in an MSID. This is
\par
a graphical structure requirement in probabilistic decision models follow-
\par
ing logical thinking in decision analysis (Robert and Terry 2001). An MSID
\par
is a huge decision model that is a set of influence diagrams or Bayesian net-
\par
works characterizing a large and complex knowledge domain. Multiple
\par
agents have consistent thinking including causal relationships on the same
\par
observation, which ensures an MSID defines a coherent decision model.
\par
Although agents' observations and decisions are interleaved with each
\par
other, their interactions follow a sequential order. The second constraint
\par
requires that nodes in a D-Sepset shared by local influence diagrams should
\par
be D-Sepnodes, which means that all parents of a public node should be
\par
included in one of local influence diagrams. This constraint allows agent's
\par
privacy and indicates that an agent can decide its decisions using local
\par
information only when the information in the D-Sepset is known. More-
\par
over, like assumptions in an MSBN, adjacent agents hold consistent com-
\par
mon information that is encoded into the same conditional probability
\par
distribution in D-Sepnodes as in an MSID. The final constraint is to ensure
\par
the compactness of MSID and HRG, and to remove any redundant infor-
\par
mation. The information, including requisite information and supporting
\par
information encoded in a D-Sepset, is just what adjacent agents are willing
\par
to share. Unnecessary information would complicate a model represen-
\par
tation and confound the necessary information.
\par
In the aforementioned SARS decision problem, each nation or com-
\par
munity can be considered as an individual agent that is modeled as a local
\par
influence diagram. Figure 3 shows the MSID that represents this multi-agent
