\par
Definition of HRG
\par
An MSID has the ability of representing a multi-agent decision problem
\par
concerning the six characters of the decision scenario. However, it does not
\par
explicitly describe the property of organizational relationships among
\par
multiple agents such as the required information for decision-making. More-
\par
over, in an uncertain and dynamic environment, agents have to be regrouped
\par
in order to adapt to new surroundings. In this process, it is the organizational
\par
relationship that guides multiple agents to compose an updated multi-agent
\par
system. In Zambonelli's work (Zambonelli, Jennings, and Wooldridge 2001),
\par
five types of organizational relationships Control, Peer, Benevolence, Depen-
\par
dency, and Ownership---between agents were discussed. However, from the
\par
viewpoint of decision-making and information flow in a multi-agent system,
\par
these relationships can be classified into two types: Control and Communi-
\par
cation. Hence, the concept of a HRG is introduced to represent the infor-
\par
mation implicated in the relationships between them.
\par
Definition 2. A HRG H is composed of three types of nodes: rectangular,
\par
triangular and oval nodes, and arcs between them. A rectangular node112 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
denotes an individual agent Aj associated with local influence diagram Ij in
\par
an MSID. A triangular node denotes the shared information C in a D-Sepset
\par
S that is required for an agent's decision D. An oval node denotes the
\par
shared information C in a D-Sepset S that is not required by any agent's
\par
decision D.
\par
With the elements in an HRG, two kinds of basic relevance graphs,
\par
called a control relevance graph and a communication relevance graph,
\par
are built as shown in Figure 4. Each is associated with a function that impli-
\par
cates an organizational relationship in the multi-agent system.
\par
