\par
MSID and HRG
\par
An MSID represents both knowledge of an environment and proper-
\par
ties of intelligent agents, while an HRG characterizes organizational
\par
FIGURE 5 The HRG for the MSID in Figure 3.114 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
relationships in a multi-agent system. Evolving over time, multiple agents
\par
are regrouped according to new relationships represented in the HRG.
\par
Consequently, the MSID is rebuilt based on the updated HRG.
\par
In an HRG, triangular and oval nodes have the exact chance nodes that
\par
can be obtained from a D-Sepset in an MSID. Hence, elements in the HRG
\par
could be driven from a well-built MSID through the Decision Bayes-ball
\par
procedure. This procedure is used to obtain required chance nodes,
\par
including required observation nodes and required probability nodes, for
\par
corresponding decision nodes. These required chance nodes and the
\par
corresponding decision nodes are elements in triangular nodes. With the
\par
exception of these nodes, other nodes in the D-Sepset belong to elements
\par
in oval nodes. Extracted from a knowledge domain, the organizational rela-
\par
tionships related with two functions are confirmed in the HRG. On another
\par
aspect, when an HRG is reorganized because of dynamic organizational
\par
relationships in the multi-agent system, it will lead to a reconstruction
\par
of the MSID, like refining (decreasing or increasing) nodes in the D-Sepset
\par
or their relevance (adding or removing arcs) to decision nodes. Accord-
\par
ingly, the MSID and HRG are regulated with each other to arrive at an
\par
elaborate framework.
\par
A compact MSID requires that a D-Sepset should be irreducible, which
\par
depends on relationships among intelligent agents. The necessary infor-
\par
mation, including requisite information and supporting information, flow-
\par
ing through a D-Sepset is just what multiple agents want to access according
\par
to their organizational relationships. An HRG is the exact model which
\par
encompasses all implications concerning the irreducibility of D-Sepset.
\par
Thus, an HRG is able to help the constraints (irreducibility of D-Sepset)
\par
checking and the reconstruction of an MSID.
\par
MODEL VERIFICATION
\par
From the definition of MSID, an MSID should satisfy three constraints:
\par
DAG structure, D-Sepet of agent interface, and irreducibility of D-Sepset.
\par
To ensure the validity of an MSID, the task of model verification has to
\par
be carried out.
\par
Verification of the DAG Structure and D-Sepset
\par
The first constraint, called DAG structure, means that there should
\par
be no directed cyclic paths in an MSID. The second constraint requires
\par
that every public node in an agent interface should be a D-Sepnode,
\par
indicating that all of its parents should be included in one of the local
\par
influence diagrams.Multi-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 115
\par
An MSID without value nodes has the same DAG property as the
\par
Theorem 1. original MSID.
\par
Proof. An MSID includes three types of nodes: chance nodes, decision
\par
nodes, and value nodes. Since a value node is a sink node without outgoing
\par
arcs in an MSID, it cannot belong to any part of a directed cyclic path. \&
\par
With Theorem 1, in the verification process of DAG structure, value
\par
nodes in an MSID can be removed safely. When value nodes are removed
\par
and decision nodes are considered as chance nodes in an MSID, the MSID
\par
has the same structure as an MSBN. In addition, as only chance nodes in an
\par
MSID are public nodes, the method of the D-Sepset verification in an MSID
\par
is similar to that in an MSBN.
\par
