\par
The modeling process is iterative until both the HRG and the MSID are
\par
acceptable.
\par
In the SARS model, the organizational relationship is defined by the
\par
involved nations' intentions. For example, to make a decision on oversea
\par
tour policies in nation 3, the information from the nation 1's SARS report
\par
is required. Hence, the Control relationship exists. In some situations,
\par
nation 3 could make this decision based on the WHO SARS report instead
\par
of the nation 1's SARS report, which relaxes its dependence on nation 1. In
\par
general, in the initialization, an HRG can be driven from a well-built MSID
\par
representing the SARS situation. After that, some small modifications with
\par
the HRG may be done based on the knowledge elicitation of the SARS situ-
\par
ation, especially on the organizational relationship among the affected
\par
nations. Finally, the accurate structure of the HRG and MSID is refined
\par
and developed. One advantage of this framework lies in its robustness
\par
and flexibility to dynamic situations. For example, when a nation is affected
\par
by the SARS virus and has to be considered in the whole community, only
\par
one new local influence diagram representing this nation is added into the
\par
model without damaging other parts of the existing MSID and HRG. This
\par
saves much effort on model reconstruction, which indicates that the frame-
\par
work of MSID and HRG is reusable.
\par
MODEL EVALUATION
\par
Evaluating a model is to determine an optimal policy for its deci-
\par
sions. The basic methods for solving an MSID are extensions of evalu-
\par
ation algorithms on a single agent-based influence diagram, such as
\par
reduction algorithms (Shachter 1986) and indirect methods (Jensen,
\par
Jensen, and Dittmer 1994). Reduction algorithms directly solve influence
\par
diagrams with some basic operations such as nodes removal and arcs
\par
reversal. Indirect methods (evaluating influence diagrams) transform
\par
an influence diagram into a rooted, strong junction tree and then solve
\par
the junction tree. As the process of transforming an MSID is similar to
\par
that in an MSBN, we would like to discuss indirect methods for solving
\par
an MSID. Furthermore, some relevant strategies to solve an MSID are
\par
investigated.118 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
Decision Solutions for MSID
\par
Solving a multi-agent decision problem is to seek the best decision for
\par
multiple agents in a cooperative and privacy protection setting. Every agent
\par
aims to obtain an optimal solution while it cooperates with its adjacent
\par
agents. An agent, without disclosing its privacy, wants to access the full
\par
and consistent public information in order to maximize its utility value.
\par
Here, the global optimal solution of the multi-agent decision problem is
\par
defined as the combination of the best decision solutions from multiple
\par
agents.
\par
An MSID represents a multi-agent decision problem with a set of local
\par
influence diagrams. These local influence diagrams are connected with
\par
D-Sepsets. Through the information in a D-Sepset, local influence diagrams
\par
could affect their adjacent ones. Hence, in the evaluation process, we
\par
should adopt some relevant strategies to avoid the incomplete and incon-
\par
sistent information in a D-Sepset. To achieve this goal, D-Sepnodes should
\par
follow a uniform elimination order in an evaluation algorithm so that they
\par
could be evaluated and removed from an MSID simultaneously. Through
\par
this way, it is ensured that an agent has obtained full, correct, and consist-
\par
ent information so that each agent's benefit is maximized in a distributed
\par
and unpredictable situation.
\par
Evaluation Algorithms
\par
Like solving influence diagrams, evaluation algorithms to solve an
\par
MSID follow two steps: transforming an MSID into a multiple-rooted cluster
\par
tree, which is a set of interconnected rooted cluster trees (Shachter 1999),
\par
and solving the multiple-rooted cluster tree.
\par
. Transforming an MSID: The methods to transform an MISD into a
\par
multiple-rooted cluster tree are combinations of evaluation algorithms
\par
in both influence diagrams and MSBN (Jensen et al. 1994; Shachter
\par
1999; Xiang 2002). Firstly, to build a modified MSID for each local influ-
\par
ence diagram, requisite observation nodes are identified using the
\par
Decision Bayes-ball procedure. Then arcs are added between identified
\par
required nodes and their corresponding decision nodes. Finally, a
\par
multiple-rooted cluster tree is built in a cooperative algorithm (Xiang
\par
2002). The basic idea of this algorithm is to transform local influence
\par
diagrams into local rooted cluster trees as well as to transform D-Sepsets
\par
into linkage trees. The linkage trees connect the transformed local,
\par
rooted cluster trees together into a multiple-rooted cluster tree. In this
\par
process, an index is introduced to show a clique sequence in the local
\par
rooted cluster tree.Multi-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 119
\par
. Solving a multiple rooted cluster tree: A multiple-rooted cluster tree is
\par
a tree structure for an MSID. It is a set of local rooted cluster trees
\par
connecting through the linkage trees. Thus, evaluating an MSID
\par
amounts to solving these local rooted cluster trees as well as the linkage
\par
trees. The local rooted cluster tree is evaluated following a partial
\par
decision order in a local influence diagram. The linkage tree among
\par
local rooted clusters coordinates the computation of adjacent cliques
\par
to make sure that D-Sepnodes are evaluated at the same time. Hence,
\par
D-Sepnodes in a linkage tree could be removed simultaneously. This
\par
has to concern a relative elimination order for D-Sepnodes in an MSID.
\par
The relative elimination order could be obtained by considering the
\par
clique index in local rooted cluster trees globally. A good relative index
\par
on cliques allows the linkage trees to be evaluated cooperatively among
\par
adjacent local rooted cluster trees.
\par
In a word, an MSID is solved in a distributed way following the above
\par
two steps. In this article, we do not focus on the process of developing
\par
a multiple-rooted cluster tree since it could be found in detail in much
\par
literature (Jensen et al. 1994; Shachter 1999; Xiang 2002). We place more
\par
emphasis on the solving of linkage trees in a multiple-rooted cluster tree,
\par
which is illustrated in the following case.
\par
Evaluation of the SARS Control Situation
\par
In Figure 3, the three influence diagrams I1, I2, and I3 are transformed
\par
into three local rooted cluster trees T1, T2, and T3, respectively. The
\par
D-Sepsets are transformed into linkage trees. For the MSID in Figure 3,
\par
the D-Sepset transformation is simple because each chance node in a
\par
D-Sepset composes one linkage tree. These local rooted cluster trees and
\par
linkage trees compose a multiple-rooted cluster tree in Figure 6.
\par
D-Sepnodes in the linkage tree adjust the solving of cliques in adjacent
\par
local cluster rooted trees since they have to be removed simultaneously.
\par
The removal of D-Sepnodes must follow an elimination order in a local
\par
cluster rooted tree. For example, in T1, nodes fa; cg have to be removed
\par
before node b, although there is no fixed elimination order for nodes
\par
fa; cg. However, in T3, the removal of node a precedes the removal of
\par
node c. Hence, a legal elimination order for D-Sepnodes in the compu-
\par
tation of the multiple-rooted cluster tree exists: node a is removed before
\par
node c, which should be removed before node b. It is also noticed that
\par
the arc associated with the linkage tree including node c is from T1 to T3
\par
in Figure 6. This follows the implication of the HRG in Figure 5. It indicates
\par
that agent A1 controls agent A3's decision d1 by the information c. For the120 Y. Zeng and K.-L. Poh
\par
FIGURE 6 A multiple-rooted cluster tree. (Nodes without color denote cliques and nodes with grey
\par
color denote linkage trees.)
\par
linkage tree among T1, T2, and T3, the arc direction depends on an elim-
\par
ination order for D-Sepnodes if this order exists; otherwise, it is optional.
\par
Following the above procedures, the MSID for the SARS model is solved
\par
by evaluating local influence diagrams individually and combining all local
\par
optimal solutions. The consistent information, implicated in the D-Sepset
\par
in an MSID and restricted in organizational relationships in an HRG, is
\par
guaranteed throughout the evaluation process. If the shared information
\par
is inconsistent, the decision-making is prone to be wrong. For example,
\par
with reference to the MSID in Figure 3, if nation 1 deliberately hides its
\par
SARS report (represented by node c), decision d1 in nation 3 will be defi-
\par
nitely wrong. Consequently, decisions in the whole community will lead
\par
to quite a large loss, which has been the case in SARS 2003. Accordingly,
\par
a true model of MSID and HRG not only provides good decisions for policy-
\par
makers, but also allows a large number of simulations to report some alerts
\par
in order to avoid a destructive loss.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
We have proposed a distributed and cooperative framework of MSID
\par
and HRG to represent a multi-agent decision problem such as the SARS
\par
control in the medical domain. It considers agency features such as the
\par
privacy protection and the dynamic organizational relationship. Model veri-
\par
fication methods assisting to build a valid model are discussed in detail. At
\par
the same time, we solve an MSID in an indirect way by transforming it into a
\par
multiple-rooted cluster tree. Some useful strategies are analyzed to obtain
\par
global optimal solutions.Multi-Agent Graphical Decision Models in Medicine 121
\par
In fact, besides the SARS situations, more practical applications exist in
\par
the medical domain. For example, in the operation of body separation, a
\par
group of doctors or experts from different medical fields are needed. Every
\par
doctor is specialized in a certain medical field, in which his=her knowledge
\par
is private. Of course, they have the same knowledge on patients' states and
\par
some common diseases. Hence, they could and should cooperate with each
\par
other to make optimal decisions in the operation process. Our proposed
\par
methodologies should be able to address this task when the evaluation
\par
approach is extended.
\par
In the real world, agents to make decisions always reside in a distributed
\par
and uncertain environment. Gathering them to hold a meeting for
\par
decision-making requires a hard coordination, such as the location and
\par
time selection. A distributed and agency modeling approach, such as the
\par
solving of MSID and HRG, has to be utilized to facilitate this decision-
\par
making. The MSID, together with the HRG, not only describes an
\par
uncertain setting of decision-making, but also characterizes properties of
\par
multiple agents. They should become important techniques to address a
\par
multi-agent decision problem.
\par
This article has solved a multi-agent decision problem roughly in
\par
principle; however; much work is required to improve the existing work
\par
such as more efficient algorithms to solve an MSID. Further research on
\par
the value of information in an MSID will enrich its theory and applications.
\par
