\par
Model practitioners increasingly place emphasis on rigorous quantitative error analysis in aquatic bio-
\par
geochemical models and the existing initiatives range from the development of alternative metrics for
\par
goodness of fit, to data assimilation into operational models, to parameter estimation techniques. How-
\par
ever, the treatment of error in many of these efforts is arguably selective and/or ad hoc. A Bayesian
\par
hierarchical framework enables the development of robust probabilistic analysis of error and uncertainty
\par
in model predictions by explicitly accommodating measurement error, parameter uncertainty, and model
\par
structure imperfection. This paper presents a Bayesian hierarchical formulation for simultaneously cali-
\par
brating aquatic biogeochemical models at multiple systems (or sites of the same system) with differences
\par
in their trophic conditions, prior precisions of model parameters, available information, measurement
\par
error or inter-annual variability. Our statistical formulation also explicitly considers the uncertainty in
\par
model inputs (model parameters, initial conditions), the analytical/sampling error associated with the
\par
field data, and the discrepancy between model structure and the natural system dynamics (e.g., miss-
\par
ing key ecological processes, erroneous formulations, misspecified forcing functions). The comparison
\par
between observations and posterior predictive monthly distributions indicates that the plankton models
\par
calibrated under the Bayesian hierarchical scheme provided accurate system representations for all the
\par
scenarios examined. Our results also suggest that the Bayesian hierarchical approach allows overcoming
\par
problems of insufficient local data by ``borrowing strength'' from well-studied sites and this feature will
\par
be highly relevant to conservation practices of regions with a high number of freshwater resources for
\par
which complete data could never be practically collected. Finally, we discuss the prospect of extend-
\par
ing this framework to spatially explicit biogeochemical models (e.g., more effectively connect inshore
\par
with offshore areas) along with the benefits for environmental management, such as the optimization
\par
of the sampling design of monitoring programs and the alignment with the policy practice of adaptive
\par
management.
