\par
ual organism physiology, population abundance, and community
\par
structure (Schindler, 1997; Straile, 2002; Weyhenmeyer, 2004).
\par
Climate forcing can have different effects on various taxonomic
\par
groups/trophic levels, and decouple species from favorable food
\par
conditions with dire consequences on ecosystem functioning
\par
(Thomas et al., 2001; Hampton, 2005). Therefore, the development
\par
of holistic understanding of the climate-driven aquatic ecosystem
\par
responses requires consideration of the complex interplay between
\par
physical, chemical factors and multiple trophic levels at a variety of
\par
spatial and temporal scales. Given the increasingly ominous con-
\par
text, the demand for reliable modeling tools that can offer insights
\par
into the ecosystem dynamics and effectively support environmen-
\par
tal management is more pressing than ever before (Arhonditsis and
\par
Brett, 2004; Arhonditsis et al., 2006). However, the general lack of
\par
uncertainty estimates for most environmental models, the arbitrary
\par
selection of higher, more costly, and often unattainable threshold
\par
values for environmental variables as a hedge against unknown\textbf{Author's personal copy}
\par
W. Zhang, G.B. Arhonditsis / Ecological Modelling 220 (2009) 2142--2161 2143
\par
prediction errors, risky model-based management decisions and
\par
unanticipated system responses are often experienced in the cur-
\par
rent management practice.
\par
Uncertainty analysis of mathematical models has been a central
\par
topic in aquatic ecosystem research, and there have been several
\par
attempts to rigorously assess model error associated with model
\par
structure and parameter uncertainty (Omlin and Reichert, 1999;
\par
Brun et al., 2001; Reichert et al., 2002; Chen et al., 2007). Model
\par
uncertainty analysis essentially aims to make inference about the
\par
joint probability distribution of model inputs, reflecting the amount
\par
of knowledge available for model parameters, initial conditions,
\par
forcing functions, and model structure. In this regard, Bayes' Theo-
\par
rem provides a convenient means to combine existing information
\par
(prior) with current observations (likelihood) for projecting future
\par
ecosystem response (posterior). Hence, the Bayesian techniques are
\par
more informative than the conventional model calibration practices
\par
(i.e., mere adjustment of model parameters until the discrepancy
\par
between model outputs and observed data is minimized), and can
\par
be used to refine our knowledge of model input parameters while
\par
obtaining predictions along with uncertainty bounds for output
\par
variables (Arhonditsis et al., 2007). Nonetheless, despite the com-
\par
pelling arguments for considering Bayesian inference techniques as
\par
an integral part of the model development process, their high com-
\par
putational demands along with the lack of analytical expressions for
\par
the posterior distributions was until recently a major impediment
\par
for their broader application (Reichert and Omlin, 1997).
\par
Elucidation of the uncertainty patterns in the multidimensional
\par
parameter spaces of mathematical models involves two critical
\par
steps: (i) selection of the likelihood function to quantify model mis-
\par
fit, and (ii) selection of the sampling scheme for generating input
\par
vectors which then are evaluated with regards to the model perfor-
\par
mance. The latter decision addresses the sampling efficiency of the
\par
approach, e.g., Random sampling, Latin hypercube, Markov chain
\par
Monte Carlo (MCMC). Many Bayesian or non-Bayesian uncertainty
\par
analysis applications (e.g., Generalized Likelihood Uncertainty Esti-
\par
mation, Bayesian Monte Carlo) have been combined with sampling
\par
algorithms which draw samples uniformly and independently from
\par
the prior parameter space. These strategies often result in Monte
\par
Carlo samples that misrepresent (or insufficiently cover) regions of
\par
high model likelihood; especially, when the joint prior parameter
\par
distribution is very wide or the parameters are highly correlated
\par
(Qian et al., 2003). To address this problem, several recent studies
\par
advocate the use of MCMC sampling schemes that are specifically
\par
designed to sample directly from the posterior distribution and to
\par
converge to the higher model likelihood regions (Gelman et al.,
\par
1995; Arhonditsis et al., 2007; Stow et al., 2007). On the other hand,
\par
the selection of the model likelihood function entails conceptual
\par
dilemmas involving the selection of generalized (e.g., root mean
\par
square error, reliability index, U-uncertainty) or purely probabilis-
\par
tic (e.g., normal, lognormal or Poisson error) likelihood functions
\par
that can significantly alter the results (Beven, 2001). In typical
\par
uncertainty analysis applications, the likelihood function is broadly
\par
specified as any measure of goodness of-fit that can be used to com-
\par
pare observed data with model predictions, e.g., sum of squared
\par
errors, fuzzy measures or even qualitative measures for model eval-
\par
uation (Franks et al., 1998; Beven, 2001; Page et al., 2004). However,
\par
it has been argued that unless the likelihood function corresponds
\par
to a formal probability distribution that directly connects the data
\par
with model input parameters and output state variables, the uncer-
\par
tainty analysis results do not have a clear Bayesian interpretation
\par
(Engeland and Gottschalk, 2002; Hong et al., 2005).
\par
In the context of water quality modeling, there are several
\par
recent studies illustrating how the Bayesian inference techniques
\par
combined with MCMC sampling schemes can improve model fore-
\par
casts and management actions over space and time. For example,
\par
Malve et al. (2005) showed how the Bayesian parameter esti-
\par
mation of a dynamic non-linear model can be used to quantify
\par
the winter respiration rates (oxygen depletion per unit area of
\par
hypolimnetic surface) in a hyper-eutrophic shallow Finnish lake.
\par
A conceptually similar modeling approach was also used to elu-
\par
cidate the confounded bottom-up and top-down effects on the
\par
phytoplankton community structure of the shallow, mesotrophic
\par
Lake Pyh\"aj\"arvi (Malve et al., 2007). Arhonditsis et al. (2007, 2008a)
\par
introduced a Bayesian calibration scheme using simple mathe-
\par
matical models ({$<$}10 state variables) and statistical formulations
\par
that explicitly accommodate measurement error, parameter uncer-
\par
tainty, and model structure error; this framework was then used to
\par
quantify the information the data contain about model inputs, to
\par
offer insights into the covariance structure among parameter esti-
\par
mates, and to obtain predictions along with credible intervals for
\par
model outputs. A follow-up study examined the efficiency of two
\par
uncertainty analysis strategies, a typical Generalized Likelihood
\par
Uncertainty Estimation (GLUE) approach combined with a random
\par
sampling scheme vis-\'a-vis a formal probabilistic model configura-
\par
tion updated with MCMC simulations, to elucidate the propagation
\par
of uncertainty through the input spaces of simple numerical aquatic
\par
biogeochemical models (Arhonditsis et al., 2008b). Finally, a recent
\par
study integrated the Bayesian calibration framework with a com-
\par
plex aquatic biogeochemical model simulating multiple elemental
\par
cycles and functional plankton groups to illustrate how the Bayesian
\par
parameter estimation can be used for assessing the exceedance
\par
frequency and confidence of compliance of different water quality
\par
criteria (Zhang and Arhonditsis, 2008).
\par
In this paper, we present another prospect of the Bayesian
\par
inference techniques by introducing a hierarchical formulation
\par
for calibrating aquatic biogeochemical models at multiple sites.
\par
This illustration is based on several synthetic datasets represent-
\par
ing oligo-, meso- and eutrophic lake conditions. Our objective
\par
is to examine if the incorporation of mathematical models into
\par
Bayesian hierarchical frameworks can assist the effective model-
\par
ing of systems with limited information by enabling the transfer of
\par
information across systems. With the hierarchical model configu-
\par
ration, we can potentially overcome problems of insufficient local
\par
data by ``borrowing strength'' from well-studied sites on the basis of
\par
distributions that connect systems in space. This outcome is highly
\par
relevant to conservation practices of regions with a high number
\par
of freshwater resources for which complete data could never be
\par
practically gathered. Finally, we discuss the prospect of extending
\par
this framework to coupled physical-biogeochemical models along
\par
with its benefits to environmental management, such as the opti-
\par
mization of the sampling design of monitoring programs and the
\par
alignment with the policy practice of adaptive management.
\par
2. Methods
\par
Hierarchical Bayes allows decomposing the environmental
\par
problems into intuitively manageable levels, thereby offering a
\par
conceptually plausible means for addressing the complexity per-
\par
vading the natural systems (Clark, 2005). As such, the Bayesian
\par
hierarchical modeling can be an indispensable methodological
\par
framework to disentangle complex ecological patterns, to exploit
\par
disparate sources of ecological information, to accommodate
\par
tightly intertwined environmental processes operating at differ-
\par
ent spatiotemporal scales, and to explicitly consider the variability
\par
pertaining to latent variables or other inherently unmeasurable
\par
quantities (Wikle, 2003a; Clark, 2005). Furthermore, Wikle (2003a)
\par
argued that rather than specifying the ecological dynamics as joint
\par
multivariate spatiotemporal covariance structures, it would also be
\par
statistically easier to factor such joint distributions into a series of
\par
conditional models, i.e., dissect the total process into a number of
\par
connected subprocesses. The essence of the Bayesian hierarchical\textbf{Author's personal copy}
\par
