\par
can be thought of as the product of the data model, specifying the
\par
dependence of the observed data on the process of interest and
\par
parameters, with the process model, describing the process condi-
\par
tional on other parameters, and the parameter model, quantifying
\par
the uncertainty in parameter values. Each of these models may then
\par
consist of multiple substages to account for the role of an inconceiv-
\par
ably complex array of environmental functions that comes into play
\par
in real world applications (Wikle, 2003a).
\par
In environmental science, the general formula (1) has been used
\par
to predict demographic processes and spatiotemporal population
\par
spread (Wikle, 2003b; Clark, 2005), to incorporate physically based
\par
prior information on simulated geophysical processes (Royle et al.,
\par
1999; Wikle et al., 2001), to stochastically treat boundary condi-
\par
tions in coupled atmospheric--ocean models (Wikle et al., 2003),
\par
and more recently to resolve the mechanisms of species coexis-
\par
tence and the biodiversity paradox (Clark et al., 2007). The present
\par
study extends the application of Bayesian hierarchical structures
\par
with process-based models, and our aim is (i) to illustrate how
\par
they can assist in sharing information among different systems (or
\par
sites); and (ii) to obtain predictions along with uncertainty bounds
\par
that take into account the insufficient amount of information in less
\par
studied systems as well as the variability observed across systems.
\par
2.1. Bayesian hierarchical framework
\par
Our statistical formulation explicitly considers the uncertainty
\par
in model inputs (model parameters, initial conditions), the ana-
\par
lytical/sampling error associated with the field data, and the
\par
discrepancy between model structure and the natural system
\par
dynamics (e.g., missing key ecological processes, erroneous formu-
\par
lations, misspecified forcing functions). Earlier applications of this
\par
formulation have resulted in an improvement of the model per-
\par
formance, i.e., the median predictions along with the 95\% credible
\par
intervals delineate zones that accurately describe the observed data
\par
(Arhonditsis et al., 2007, 2008a,b). In this study, the Bayesian hier-
\par
archical framework builds upon the assumption that the model
\par
discrepancy is invariant with the input conditions, and thus the
\par
difference between model and system dynamics is constant over
\par
the annual cycle for each state variable. The hierarchical struc-
\par
tures examined consist of two submodels representing two local
\par
aquatic systems (or two sites of the same system) with differences
\par
in their trophic conditions, prior precisions of model parameters,
\par
available information, measurement error or inter-annual variabil-
\par
ity (Table 1). In particular, the first scenario considers a mesotrophic
\par
system combined with an oligotrophic or eutrophic one, aiming
\par
to examine the posterior patterns when crossing different trophic
\par
states under the hierarchical framework. Two mesotrophic datasets
\par
with different inter-annual variability (15\% and 30\%) were used in
\par
scenario B, thereby assessing the robustness of the results if, for
\par
example, we explicitly consider both dynamic (inshore) and static
\par
(offshore) areas of the same system during the model calibration
\par
process. The focus of the third scenario was to compare how the two
\par
submodels will be calibrated when combining systems (or sites)
\par
with different sampling intensity (C1 and C2) or systems with dif-
