2.2. Mathematical model
\par
We used a zero-dimensional (single compartment) model that
\par
considers the flows of mass among four state variables: phosphate
\par
(PO4), phytoplankton (PHYT), zooplankton (ZOOP), and detritus
\par
(DET). The mathematical description of the aquatic biogeochem-
\par
ical model is provided in the Appendix A (Table A1), while the
\par
definition of the model parameters can be found in Arhonditsis\textbf{Author's personal copy}
\par
2146 W. Zhang, G.B. Arhonditsis / Ecological Modelling 220 (2009) 2142--2161
\par
et al. (2007, 2008b). The phosphate equation considers the phy-
\par
toplankton uptake, the proportion of the zooplankton excretion
\par
and mortality/predation that is returned back to the system as dis-
\par
solved phosphorus. Epilimnetic phosphate levels are also fuelled by
\par
the bacteria-mediated mineralization of detritus, exogenous load-
\par
ing, and are subject to seasonally varying diffusive mixing with
\par
the hypolimnion. The equation for phytoplankton biomass consid-
\par
ers phytoplankton production and losses due to basal metabolism,
\par
settling and herbivorous zooplankton grazing. The growth of phy-
\par
toplankton is regulated from the physical (light and temperature)
\par
conditions and the phosphorus availability. Phytoplankton and
\par
detritus are two alternative food sources of zooplankton with equal
\par
palatability. Both herbivory and detrivory were formulated using
\par
the Holling Type III function, and a sigmoid closure term was
\par
selected to represent a ``switchable'' type of predator behaviour
\par
controlled by a prey threshold concentration (Edwards and Yool,
\par
2000). The particulate phosphorus (detritus) is fuelled by phyto-
\par
plankton respiration, a fraction of the zooplankton growth that
\par
represents the faecal pellets, and exogenous loading. Detritus
\par
is transformed to phosphate by seasonally forced mineraliza-
\par
tion processes and sinks out of the epilimnion at a constant
\par
rate.
\par
The well-studied system (submodel 1) in the hierarchical model
\par
configuration was represented from the average Lake Washing-
\par
ton conditions; a mesotrophic system with limnological processes
\par
strongly dominated by a recurrent spring diatom bloom with epil-
\par
imnetic chlorophyll concentration peaks on average at 10 g L-1
\par
,
\par
which is approximately three times higher than the summer con-
\par
centrations when the system is phosphorus limited (Arhonditsis
\par
et al., 2003). The hypothetical systems in the second submodel
\par
represent oligotrophic conditions, mesotrophic conditions in less
\par
intensively studied sites, and eutrophic conditions with monomic-
\par
tic or dimictic mixing patterns, which exchange information via
\par
the hierarchical structure with the first submodel. In our anal-
\par
ysis, the average input total phosphorus (TP) concentrations for
\par
the oligo-, meso-, and eutrophic environments correspond to 50
\par
(32.5 g TP L-1), 100 (65 g TP L-1), and 200\% (130 g TP L-1) of
\par
the reference conditions in Lake Washington, respectively. Based
\par
on these loading scenarios, the model was run using the poste-
\par
rior medians presented in Arhonditsis et al. (2008b). The simulated
\par
monthly averages provided the mean values of normal distribu-
\par
tions with standard deviations assigned to be 15\% of the monthly
\par
values for each state variable (Zhang and Arhonditsis, 2008). These
\par
distributions were then sampled to generate the oligo-, meso- and
\par
eutrophic datasets used for the Bayesian model calibration along
\par
with the corresponding hypolimnetic phosphate boundary condi-
\par
tions.
\par
2.3. Numerical approximations for posterior distributions
\par
The calibration vector consists of the same 14 parameters used
\par
in previous applications of the model (Arhonditsis et al., 2007,
\par
2008b). The prior distributions of the hyperparameters or global
\par
priors were formulated on the basis of existing knowledge (e.g.,
\par
field observations, laboratory studies, literature information and
\par
expert judgment) of the relative plausibility of their values. In this
\par
study, we identified the global minimum and maximum values
\par
for each parameter, and then we assigned lognormal distributions
\par
parameterized such that 95\% of the parameter values were lying
\par
within the literature ranges (Steinberg et al., 1997). The global prior
\par
distributions of the model parameters are presented in Table 2.
\par
The numerical approximations of the posterior distributions were
\par
obtained using the general normal-proposal Metropolis algorithm
\par
along with an ordered overrelaxation (Spiegelhalter et al., 2003).
\par
This MCMC scheme generates multiple samples per iteration and
\par
reduces the within-chain correlations by selecting a value that is
\par
negatively correlated with the current one of each stochastic node
\par
(Neal, 1998). The posterior simulations were based on one chain
\par
with starting point a vector obtained from an earlier optimiza-
\par
tion of the model with the Fletcher--Reeves conjugate-gradient
\par
method (Chapra and Canale, 1998). We used 50,000 iterations
\par
and convergence was assessed with the modified Gelman--Rubin
\par
convergence statistic (Brooks and Gelman, 1998). Our framework
\par
was implemented in the WinBUGS Differential Interface (WBD-
\par
iff); an interface that allows numerical solution of systems of
\par
ordinary differential equations (ODEs) within the WinBUGS soft-
\par
ware. The ODEs were solved using the fourth-order Runge-Kutta
\par
method.
\par
