4. Discussion
\par
The philosophical and pragmatic differences between Bayesian
\par
and frequentist methods of inference have been extensively
\par
debated in the ecological literature (Dennis, 1996; Ellison, 1996,
\par
2004). The distinctions arise from the different definitions of
\par
probability (long-run relative frequencies of events vis-\'a-vis an
\par
individual's degree of belief in the likelihood of an event), the
\par
use of prior knowledge along with the sample data, and the
\par
treatment of model parameters as random variables or as fixed
\par
quantities (Ellison, 2004). Recently, however, Clark (2003, 2005)
\par
offered a different perspective arguing that the assumptions of fre-
\par
quentist and simple Bayesian models are more similar than are
\par
usually perceived in ecological studies and that only the hierarchi-
\par
cal Bayes is a distinctly different framework to accommodate the
\par
complexity in environmental systems. Hierarchical Bayes relaxes
\par
the fundamental assumption that there is an underlying ``true''\textbf{Author's personal copy}
\par
W. Zhang, G.B. Arhonditsis / Ecological Modelling 220 (2009) 2142--2161 2153
\par
Fig. 5. (Continued ).
\par
parameter value that is gradually approximated with increasing
\par
sample size. Using simple empirical models with a large num-
\par
ber of hierarchies and spatiotemporally variant parameters, the
\par
latter feature has been shown to provide an effective means for
\par
addressing difficult space-time problems (Borsuk et al., 2001;
\par
Wikle, 2003a; Malve and Qian, 2006). Our objective herein was
\par
to demonstrate how the hierarchical Bayes can be used to simul-
\par
taneously calibrate mathematical models at multiple sites with
\par
different ecological dynamics or amount of information avail-
\par
able.
