4.1. framework?
\par
How robust are the posterior patterns under the hierarchical
\par
The degree of updating of the model input parameters from prior
\par
to posterior is usually evaluated using three different criteria: (i)
\par
shift in the most likely value; (ii) reduction in the parameter uncer-
\par
tainty; and (iii) change in the shape of the distribution (Endres and
\par
Schindelin, 2003). In this study, although we have not quantified
\par
the change in the shape from prior to posterior parameter distribu-
\par
tions, the first two criteria did reveal interesting results with regards
\par
to the degree of updating under the hierarchical model configura-
\par
tion. First, we highlight the often significant shifts of the posterior
\par
means and the increased standard deviations of the zooplankton
\par
mortality rate (d), the half-saturation constant for predation (pred),
\par
and the zooplankton grazing half-saturation constant ( ). These
\par
posterior patterns probably indicate that the zooplankton feeding
\par
kinetics and the mortality/higher predation rates (the so-called clo-
\par
sure term) are primarily used to accommodate the site-specific
\par
variability, and therefore can be more resistant to the Bayesian
\par
shrinkage effect. Shrinkage is a well-known phenomenon in both
\par
Bayesian and frequentist statistics in which individual estimates
\par
are shrunk toward the overall mean when they are derived jointly
\par
rather than independently (Efron and Morris, 1975; Gelman and
\par
Pardoe, 2006). This finding reiterates the well-documented pro-
\par
found impact of these parameters upon the dynamics of plankton
\par
ecosystem models and underscores the importance of developing
\par
articulate site-specific prior probability distributions when data
\par
from different study sites are combined under the hierarchical\textbf{Author's personal copy}
