4.2. How can the hierarchical framework assist the current
\par
modeling practices?
\par
The Bayesian hierarchical proposition may be useful for a variety
\par
of aquatic science and ecological modeling applications in which
\par
partial, but not complete, commonality can be assumed among
\par
the modeled units. A characteristic case is the Laurentian Great
\par
Lakes region where the most degraded areas are nearshore zones
\par
above the summer thermocline adjacent to the mouths of large
\par
rivers and enclosed bays/harbours with restricted mixing with
\par
offshore water. These areas are intermediate zones in that they
\par
receive highly polluted inland waters from watersheds with signif-
\par
icant agricultural, urban, and/or industrial activities while mixing
