\par
fully accommodated by the typical practice of developing spatially
\par
explicit mechanistic models with common parameter values over
\par
the entire systems; that is, how realistic is to assume that the same
\par
phytoplankton growth rate occurs throughout the waterbody?
\par
Rather, the practical compromise between entirely site-specific and
\par
globally common parameter estimates offered by the hierarchi-
\par
cal approach may be a conceptually more sound strategy (Fig. 8).
\par
Importantly, our illustration showed that such model configuration
\par
does not negate the basic premise for using process-based models,
\par
i.e., reproduction of the observed system dynamics while gaining
\par
mechanistic insights, and it does provide parameter posteriors that
\par
have meaningful ecological interpretation. For example, the poste-
\par
