\par
work to provide a mechanism for pooling information from systems
\par
with different sampling intensity and strengthen the predictive
\par
ability in individual sites. Indeed, our analysis showed that primar-
\par
ily the scenario that uses seasonal averages for the state variables
\par
(C1) and secondarily the one using data solely from the strati-
\par
fied period (C2) resulted in relatively similar posterior parameter
\par
moments between the two locations, providing more confidence in
\par
the representation of the ecological structure of the less intensively
\par
studied site. Furthermore, aside from the previously mentioned
\par
low precisions of the parameters associated with the zooplank-
\par
ton feeding kinetics and mortality rates, the differences between
\par
the posterior first and second order moments of the hyperparam-
\par
eters and the system-specific parameters were also quite small
\par
(Fig. 9); that is, the site-specific parameters converged toward the
\par
global means but these shifts were not accompanied by a signifi-
\par
cant shrinkage of the corresponding parameter standard deviations
\par
(Gelman and Pardoe, 2006).
\par
The comparison between the observed and posterior predic-
\par
tive monthly distributions along with the values of the error
\par
terms can also be used to dictate the optimal type of informa-
\par
tion required to improve the predictive power of the model. For
\par
example, the calibration of the model with data collected once
\par
per season results in very wide uncertainty bands and thus less
\par
useful for water quality management. Furthermore, although the
\par
median predictions closely match the majority of the observed
\par
data and the values of the error terms were notably lower, the
\par
model fails to capture the timing of the spring phytoplankton
\par
bloom and does not reproduce the contemporaneous peak of the
\par
zooplankton biomass. Interestingly, the latter problems were not
\par
alleviated when the model of the less intensively studied system
\par
was combined with a ``refined'' model parameterization based on
\par
a well-studied site (scenario E). On the other hand, the use of data
\par
collected with higher frequency but solely from the stratified period
\par
overcomes the misrepresentation of the spring plankton dynamics
\par
and also reduces the predictive uncertainty. Similar experiments
\par
can be designed regarding the spatiotemporal sampling intensity
\par
or the collection of data on parameters versus data on output
\par
variables, while the subsequent assessment of the value of infor-
\par
mation can further optimize the existing monitoring programs and
\par
assist model-based decision making and management (Dorazio
\par
and Johnson, 2003). Finally, we note the overwhelmingly high
\par
error values resulting from a hierarchical structure that combines
\par
a mesotrophic monomictic with an eutrophic dimictic system.
\par
These results are not surprising as it would seem counterintu-
\par
itive to improve ecological forecasts by exchanging information
\par
between systems that have substantial functional and structural
\par
differences. This numerical experiment merely aimed to provide
\par
an additional sensitivity analysis with regards to the role of the
\par
different parameters in accommodating the variability of the two
\par
datasets.
\par
4.3. Outstanding methodological issues and future perspectives
\par
In conclusion, we introduced a Bayesian hierarchical framework
\par
that enables the development of robust probabilistic analysis of
\par
error and uncertainty in model predictions by explicitly taking
\par
into account the measurement error, parameter uncertainty, and
\par
model structure imperfection. Our intent was to illustrate how this
\par
approach can be used to transfer knowledge in space, and therefore
\par
to simultaneously calibrate process-based models at multiple sites.
\par
Some of the unresolved technical aspects and future perspectives
\par
of the Bayesian hierarchical scheme are as follows:
\par
(i) Hierarchical Bayes and spatially explicit mathematical models:
\par
The description of spatial and spatiotemporal environmental
\par
processes has been the focus of several Bayesian modeling
\par
studies, and the existing propositions involve general hierar-
\par
chical spatial model frameworks (Cressie, 2000; Wikle, 2003a),
\par
Markov random field models (Besag et al., 1995), hierarchical
\par
spatio-temporal models (Wikle et al., 1998), spatiotempo-
\par
ral dynamic models (Wikle et al., 2001), and spatiotemporal
\par
models that are simplified by dimension reduction (Berliner
\par
et al., 2000) or by conditioning on processes considered
\par
to be latent or hidden (Hughes and Guttorp, 1994). In the\textbf{Author's personal copy}
\par
