\par
present study, we advocated the relaxation of the assump-
\par
tion of globally common parameter values used in coupled
\par
physical-biogeochemical models and the adoption of hier-
\par
archical statistical formulations reflecting the more realistic
\par
notion that each site is unique but shares some common-
\par
ality of behaviour with other sites of the same system. The
\par
proposed hierarchical structure will be easily employed with
\par
model segmentations of 5--10 completely mixed boxes with-
\par
out significant increase of the computation demands, while
\par
future research should also evaluate formulations that explic-
\par
itly consider the spatiotemporal dependence patterns of the
\par
parameter values and model error terms.
\par
(ii) Mathematical models fitted to cross-system data: In aquatic
\par
ecosystem modeling, cross-system data have been used in a
\par
global sense to develop empirical relationships between catch-
\par
ment features and nutrient loading (Howarth et al., 1996), lake
\par
morphometric/hydraulic characteristics and total phospho-
\par
rus concentrations (Brett and Benjamin, 2008), light/nutrient
\par
availability and phytoplankton levels (Smith, 1986; Malve
\par
and Qian, 2006), algal and zooplankton biomass (McCauley
\par
and Kalff, 1981). In this context, the proposed framework is
\par
a logical advancement that allows developing models with
\par
stronger mechanistic foundation while remaining within the
\par
bounds of data-based parameter estimation (Borsuk et al.,
\par
2001). The main advantage of such hierarchical model con-
\par
figuration will be the effective modeling of systems with
\par
limited information by borrowing strength from well-studied
\par
systems. Indeed, our analysis showed that this approach
\par
provides ecologically meaningful parameter estimates at loca-
\par
tions with limited data as well as site-specific predictions
\par
with more realistic uncertainty ranges than the conventional
\par
pooled approaches. Future research should identify the most
\par
appropriate criteria (trophic status, morphological charac-
\par
teristics) for delineating the number of levels and type of
\par
groups included in the hierarchical structures, thereby opti-
\par
mizing the transfer of information across systems, e.g., see
\par
the geomorphological typology presented in Malve and Qian
\par
(2006).
\par
(iii) Prior assumptions on parameter distributions and model struc-
\par
ture: The prior distributions assigned to the hyperparameters
\par
of hierarchical models have received considerable attention
\par
in the Bayesian literature (Box and Tiao, 1973; Spiegelhalter
\par
et al., 2003; Gelman et al., 1995), and special emphasis has
\par
been placed on the use of appropriate noninformative priors
\par
for hierarchical variance parameters (Gelman, 2005). In the
\par
context of the present analysis, we also caution to carefully
\par
select the priors for the error terms representing the discrep-
\par
ancy between the model structure and the natural system
\par
dynamics, as our experience was that some of the results pre-
\par
sented herein were sensitive to the pertinent selection. We
\par
also note the instrumental role of the parameters associated
\par
with the zooplankton feeding kinetics and the mortality/higher
\par
predation rates in accommodating the site-specific variabil-
\par
ity. In this regard, an appealing next step would be the
\par
development of a prescriptive approach for optimizing the
\par
structure of hierarchical modeling constructs by choosing site
\par
specific closure terms (linear, quadratic, hyperbolic, sigmoid)
\par
or functional forms for zooplankton grazing (linear, saturat-
\par
ing, saturating with feeding threshold, acclimating to ambient
\par
food) on the basis of the posterior parameter and model output
\par
patterns.
