1. Introduction
\par
Over the past few decades, the massive production of construction and demolition
\par
waste (CDW) is increasingly threatening the public environment and human health glob-
\par
ally [1]. Although people have gradually realized the urgency of promoting the recycling
\par
of construction and demolition waste, the development of CDW treatment is not well
\par
developed in China [2]. Existing research indicates that the relatively low resource uti-
\par
lization of CDW in China is due to the lack of a developed CDW recycling market [3].
\par
Additionally, the fairly low level of development of the CDW recycling market suggests
\par
that considerable time and money must be invested in building relationships and tracking
\par
pricing changes [4]. However, the difficulty of building collaborative relationships has been
\par
Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878. https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19073878 https://www.mdpi.com/journal/ijerphInt. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878 2 of 22
\par
a problem for enterprises, hindering their efforts to improve their overall performance [5].
\par
The resulting impact has made it difficult to achieve the high-quality development of CDW
\par
resource utilization supply chains. In China, CDW accounts for about 30--40\% of the total
\par
amount of urban waste, while its resource utilization rate is less than 5\%; but in developed
\par
countries such as Japan and the European Union, its utilization has exceeded 90\% [6,7].
\par
In recent years, various issues that are related to CDW management have attracted
\par
extensive academic attention. Many scholars have studied the properties of CDW itself and
\par
those of the remanufactured products that are produced from construction and demolition
\par
waste [8--12]. Some scholars have also studied the application of green development
\par
concepts such as circular economy theory, 3R principles and whole life cycle strategies in
\par
CDW management [2,13,14]. While these academics have made significant contributions
\par
to the development of CDW management, there are still some problems that are yet to
\par
be resolved. In the construction field, remanufacturers recycle CDW both directly from
\par
building material consumers and indirectly from CDW recyclers. As a result, there is both
\par
a cooperative relationship and a conflicting relationship between the remanufacturer and
\par
the CDW recycler, between channels.
\par
The management of this channel conflict affects the success of dual-channel supply
\par
chains [15]. Channel conflict among channel members tends to be a very negative force
\par
and will result in the less efficient recycling of construction and demolition waste. If we
\par
cannot eliminate the problem of channel conflict, it will be difficult to improve the efficiency
\par
of CDW recycling. Furthermore, the dual-channel reverse supply chain focuses on the
\par
integration of logistics, capital flow and information flow on the reverse chain, which is
\par
conducive to helping enterprises to overcome the conflicts between channels. Therefore,
\par
the concept of dual-channel reverse supply chain management is very suitable for CDW
\par
management. However, few scholars have optimized the CDW management model from
\par
the perspective of a dual-channel reverse supply chain operation.
\par
Information sharing is widely recognized as an important tool for coordinating supply
\par
chain activities, a key factor in reducing supply chain costs and a major means of improving
\par
supply chain performance [16--18]. In the construction field, the uncertainty of market
\par
demand significantly increases the decision risk for supply chain stakeholders. Therefore,
\par
it is particularly important to forecast market demand information. As the CDW recycler is
\par
closer to the consumer market, their market information can be obtained in order to more
\par
effectively predict the market demand. Effective information sharing will significantly
\par
enhance the effectiveness of supply chain practices and further contribute to good supply
\par
chain performance [19--21]. Therefore, information sharing by the recycler should be
\par
considered in the decision-making process of the CDW resource utilization supply chain.
\par
Therefore, studying the impact of recyclers' information sharing on the performance
\par
of CDW resource utilization supply chain is of great significance for the development of a
\par
circular economy. In particular, this paper not only supplements the field of supply chain
\par
performance research with evidence from CDW resource utilization, but it also expands the
\par
field of circular economy development research from the perspective of information sharing.
\par
To sum up, this paper focuses on the impact of recyclers' information sharing on the
\par
performance of the construction waste resource utilization supply chain and focuses on
\par
answering the following three questions: First, how will recyclers' information sharing
\par
affect the decisions and profits of the construction and demolition waste resource utilization
\par
supply chain? Second, in the construction and demolition waste resource utilization
\par
supply chain, how can a recycler benefit from information sharing? Third, how can a
\par
remanufacturer stimulate recyclers' information sharing?
\par
In the context of information asymmetry and information sharing, this paper studies
\par
the impact of recyclers' sharing their market demand forecasting information on the perfor-
\par
mance of the construction and demolition waste resource utilization supply chain. This
\par
paper mainly uses the Bayesian Nash equilibrium theory in order to reveal the impact of
\par
the information sharing decision of the recycler in the construction and demolition waste
\par
resource utilization supply chain and it investigates the conditions of information sharingInt. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878 3 of 22
\par
from a recycler in the construction and demolition waste resource utilization supply chain.
\par
At the same time, when the recycler is unwilling to share their information, a coordination
\par
mechanism was designed in order to encourage the recycler to share their information, in
\par
order to achieve a win--win situation with the remanufacturer.
\par
The rest of this paper is structured as follows. Section 2 provides a relevant literature
\par
review; Section 3 presents a description of the problem and the associated assumptions;
\par
Section 4 establishes and solves the CDW resource utilization supply chain decision under
\par
the recyclers' information sharing and information non-sharing conditions; Section 5
\par
provides an analysis of this conditional model; Section 6 provides numerical and sensitivity
\par
analyses and, finally, Section 7 summarizes the conclusions and limitations of this paper.
\par
2. Literature Review
\par
In this section, this paper reviews the previous literature which pertains to three
\par
relevant aspects, as shown in Table 1. Firstly, this paper reviews the related research
\par
and latest progress of CDW management from the perspective of supply chain operation.
\par
Second, the definition and related literature on the dual-channel reverse supply chain
\par
(DRSC) are comprehensively reviewed. Finally, the research on the impact of information
\par
sharing on the supply chain's performance and the research on the information sharing
\par
strategies of information holders are reviewed.
\par
