2.1. CDW Management from the Perspective of Supply Chain Operation
\par
The existing research on CDW management from the perspective of supply chain
\par
operations can be divided into two categories: closed-loop supply chains and reverse
\par
supply chains.
\par
2.1.1. Closed-Loop Supply Chain Aspects
\par
Li et al. [7] studied the behavioral decision-making problem of a closed-loop supply
\par
chain of CDW consisting of manufacturers, recyclers and retailers and pointed out that the
\par
concept of closed-loop supply chain management is very suitable for CDW management.
\par
Su et al. [22] built a closed-loop supply chain for CDW consisting of remanufacturers,
\par
manufacturers and recyclers in order to study the incentive mechanism of CDW recycling
\par
under the condition of information asymmetry. It should be noted that the informationInt. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878 4 of 22
\par
asymmetry in their research specifically refers to a moral hazard, which is not the same
\par
concept as the demand-related information that is investigated in the present paper.
\par
2.1.2. Reverse Supply Chain Aspects
\par
Hu et al. [23] proposed a CDW reverse supply chain consisting of manufacturers,
\par
recyclers and contractors. Based on this, they analyzed a dynamic game strategy for CDW
\par
recycling with learning and reference effects. Su et al. [24] developed a reverse supply chain
\par
of CDW consisting of contractors, recyclers and landfills in order to study the charging
\par
decisions for recyclers and landfills under different rights structures.
\par
The above studies have fully proved that it is feasible to solve the problem of CDW
\par
management from the perspective of supply chain operation and the concept of supply
\par
chain management is suitable for application to CDW management.
\par
Existing studies have studied CDW from the perspective of closed-loop supply chain
\par
operations, but these studies have not considered the role of information sharing [38,39].
\par
In this paper, we investigate the impact of recyclers' information sharing on the perfor-
\par
mance of CDW resource utilization supply chains from a microscopic perspective. In the
\par
actual operation of the CDW supply chain, the recycling and remanufacturing (resource
\par
utilization) of CDW and the sales of CDW-based remanufactured products are two rela-
\par
tively independent processes. Therefore, it is more appropriate to study the CDW resource
\par
utilization supply chain from the perspective of a reverse supply chain operation.
\par
Although the above-mentioned scholars have conducted in-depth research on CDW
\par
management from the perspective of a supply chain operation, they are all limited to
\par
offline recycling channels. They did not consider the related issues in the DRSC with
\par
the introduction of online recycling channels. However, in reality, there are many cases
\par
where remanufacturers open online recycling channels. Therefore, this paper introduces the
\par
reverse supply chain of online-and-offline dual recycling channels into CDW management
\par
for the first time.
\par
2.2. DRSC
\par
The dual-channel recycling model was first proposed in a study of closed-loop supply
\par
chains. Giri et al. [25] proposed a closed-loop supply chain model involving manufacturers,
\par
retailers and third parties, with manufacturers conducting their recycling activities through
\par
their own e-tail channels and third-party channels. Liu et al. [26] studied the pricing
\par
and reverse channel selection decision problems that exist in a closed-loop supply chain
\par
and found that a dual-channel recycling model is the optimal choice for manufacturers.
\par
It was not long before dual-channel reverse supply chains were discussed in articles
\par
in mainstream journals. Feng et al. [27] first introduced the concept of online recycling
\par
channels into the traditional reverse supply chain in 2017 and they constructed profit models
\par
for remanufacturers and recyclers under centralized decision-making and decentralized
\par
decision-making according to the different preferences of consumers. Chen et al. [28] first
\par
defined a DRSC in 2018 as a supply chain model in which online and offline recycling
\par
channels coexist. So far, the DRSC has been clearly explained: it is a two-level reverse
\par
supply chain system that is composed of remanufacturers and recyclers, in which the
\par
remanufacturers carry out recycling activities through dual online and offline channels.
\par
So far, scholars have carried out research on the contract design, pricing strategy
\par
and network design of DRSC. Wu et al. [29] conducted research on the DRSC contract
\par
coordination problem considering the service level and they then established an effective
\par
revenue-sharing contract. Jin et al. [30] analyzed the influence of the power structure on
\par
the pricing decisions and profits of remanufacturers and recyclers in a DRSC. By incorpo-
\par
rating consumers' preference for online recycling channels into the system, Li and Wu [31]
\par
conducted research on the strategic network design of a DRSC and constructed a detailed
\par
quantitative model for network design optimization.
\par
Although the above-mentioned scholars have conducted in-depth research on the
\par
decision-making of DRSC, the impact of demand information sharing has been neglected.Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878 5 of 22
\par
However, in the DRSC (i.e., a CDW resource utilization supply chain) in the context of the
\par
construction industry, the uncertainty of the market's demand information greatly hinders
\par
the scientific decision-making of the remanufacturers, which has a further serious impact
\par
on supply chain performance. Therefore, the impact of market demand information sharing
\par
on the supply chain of CDW resource utilization cannot be ignored and should be fully
\par
researched and revealed.
\par
2.3. Information Sharing
\par
With the continuous development of the field of supply chain management, the impact
\par
of demand information sharing on the supply chain has been increasingly recognized and
\par
investigated by the academic community. The research that is related to this paper includes
\par
the influence of information sharing on supply chain performance and the influence of the
\par
decision-making of information holders in a supply chain on information sharing.
\par
Aspects of the Impact of Information Sharing on Supply Chain Performance
\par
Lee et al. [32] explored how to quantify the benefits of sharing more demand infor-
\par
mation in a two-tier supply chain system consisting of retailers and manufacturers. They
\par
demonstrated that information sharing leads to better reductions in the supply chain's costs
\par
and inventory levels. Cheng and Wu [33] considered the impact of information sharing
\par
on the inventory and expected costs in a two-tier supply chain with multiple retailers
\par
and found that the manufacturers' expected costs and inventory levels decreased as the
\par
level of information sharing increased. From the above literature, one can conclude that
\par
information sharing can improve the performance of the supply chain. However, these
\par
related studies are based on the perspective of a positive supply chain. Although Wang
\par
et al. [34] came to a similar conclusion from the perspective of a dual-channel closed-loop
\par
supply chain, it has not yet been revealed whether the same conclusion can be drawn for
\par
the DRSC of CDW resource utilization supply chain.
\par
Scholars usually hold two views on the decision-making of information holders in
\par
the supply chain regarding information sharing. The traditional view holds that the
\par
information holders will always suffer a loss of benefits due to their information sharing
\par
and that they will not share their information voluntarily. For example: Li [35] analyzed the
\par
impact of vertical information sharing on the profit and social benefits of the supply chain
\par
in a two-level supply chain that was composed of a manufacturer and multiple retailers
\par
and their results showed that the retailers never share their market demand information.
\par
Huang et al. [36] considered the value of information sharing in a dual-channel closed-
\par
loop supply chain consisting of manufacturers, retailers and collectors and found that the
\par
retailers' information sharing would not increase or even reduce their own profits. Another
\par
view is that, in some cases, information holders will benefit from information sharing. For
\par
example: Guan et al. [37] explored the problem of sharing demand information in two
\par
competing supply chains and they found that the retailers would voluntarily share demand
\par
information if the manufacturer's service investment was more efficient. This paper will
\par
further investigate this issue in the CDW resource utilization supply chain.
\par
None of the above studies have examined CDW management from the perspective of
\par
DRSC operations. In addition, no scholars have studied the impact of demand information
\par
sharing on the DRSC. Therefore, the main contributions of this paper have two aspects.
\par
First, while there have been studies on CDW management from a supply chain operational
\par
perspective, these studies were not conducted in the context of a DRSC and did not take
\par
into account the issue of information sharing. However, these problems do in fact exist and
\par
the research that is presented within this paper aims to solve these problems. Secondly,
\par
according to the characteristics of the CDW resource utilization supply chain, a Stackelberg
\par
game model was constructed and the Bayesian equilibrium solution and expected profit
\par
under the two conditions of information sharing or not sharing were obtained for a com-
\par
parative analysis. This paper then summarizes the rules that recyclers should follow in
\par
their decision-making regarding information sharing as remanufacturers' environmentalg , p
