\par
level, in order to obtain greater benefits with limited capital investment by reducing their
\par
environmental responsibility costs.
\par
Second, in the CDW resource utilization supply chain, because recyclers are closer
\par
to the consumer market, they can obtain market information and predict market demand
\par
more effectively. If recyclers share their demand forecast information with remanufacturers
\par
free of charge, remanufacturers will make scientific and reasonable decisions on pricing
\par
and their own environmental responsibilities; as such their profits will always increase due
\par
to information sharing.
\par
Third, whether recyclers have an incentive to share their demand forecast information
\par
with the remanufacturer and whether the recycler should charge for the information depend
\par
on the efficiency of the remanufacturer's environmental responsibility investments. When
\par
the environmental responsibility investment efficiency of the remanufacturer is in a high
\par
range, the recycler will obtain higher income due to their information sharing. In this
\par
situation, the recycler is motivated to share their information for free; in other situations,
\par
their information will not be shared for free.
\par
Fourth, for the CDW resource utilization supply chain as a whole, the impact of
\par
information sharing by recyclers depends on the intensity of the competition among the
\par
channels, the market share of offline recycling channels and the efficiency of the recyclers'
\par
environmental responsibility investment. In most cases, recyclers' information sharing is
\par
beneficial to the overall profit increase in the CDW resource utilization supply chain or it
\par
has no significant impact on the profit. However, when the efficiency of the environmental
\par
responsibility investment of remanufacturers is too high or the market share of offline
\par
recycling channels is large, recyclers' information sharing will cause losses to the entire
\par
CDW resource utilization supply chain.
\par
Fifth, when recyclers are reluctant to share their information, remanufacturers can
\par
take two effective measures to encourage the recyclers to share their information, thereby
\par
increasing their own profits and improving the performance of the entire CDW resource
\par
utilization supply chain. Namely: (1) remanufacturers can properly improve the efficiency
\par
of their environmental responsibility investment, so that the recyclers can increase their
\par
income due to their information sharing, thereby urging the recyclers to share their infor-
\par
mation and (2) the remanufacturer can pay a certain information fee to the recycler, so that
\par
the profit that is lost by the recycler due to their information sharing can be compensated,
\par
thereby encouraging the recycler to share their demand forecast information.
\par
7.2. Implications
\par
This research enables companies that are engaged in CDW recycling and remanufac-
\par
turing business to have a more comprehensive and rich understanding of the impact of
\par
recyclers' information sharing behavior on supply chain performance. This study also
\par
provides important management implications for the government and companies in the
\par
CDW resource utilization supply chain, namely:
\par
(1) For remanufacturers, there is always a benefit from the sharing of information in the
\par
CDW resource utilization supply chain. Therefore, remanufacturers should strive to
\par
obtain demand forecast information from recyclers. When the cost of obtaining the
\par
information is less than the increase in income, that is, when the marginal income
\par
is greater than zero, a certain fee can be paid to the recycler in order to obtain their
\par
demand forecast information and improve the remanufacturer's own profit. Alterna-
\par
tively, the remanufacturer can assume the appropriate environmental responsibility
\par
investment efficiency to achieve a win--win situation with the recycler, prompting the
\par
recycler to share their demand forecast information.
\par
(2) For recyclers, when information is shared, the level of the environmental responsibility
\par
of the remanufacturer can be affected by controlling the degree of their optimism or
\par
pessimism within their market demand forecast information, thereby affecting the
\par
efficiency of the remanufacturer's environmental responsibility investment, so as to
\par
increase their own profits.Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2022, 19, 3878 20 of 22
\par
(3) For the government, in order to promote the efficient and sustainable development of
\par
the CDW resource utilization industry, relevant policies should be introduced in order
\par
to stimulate the overall profit of the supply chain. Specifically, corresponding policies
\par
should be introduced that require the remanufacturer's environmental responsibility
\par
input, so as to avoid the remanufacturer's environmental responsibility input cost
\par
coefficient being too low. In order to achieve an effective level of macro-control, the
\par
government must also encourage the remanufacturer to use online channels to recycle
\par
CDW and adopt policies such as raising the taxes on offline CDW recycling channels
\par
in order to avoid offline recycling channels occupying too much market share.
\par
Although this study has involved analysis which aims to examine the impact of
\par
recyclers' information sharing on the supply chain of CDW resource utilization, it has
\par
certain limitations. For example, although the game model that is constructed in this paper
\par
theoretically explains the impact of information sharing on the performance of the CDW
\par
resource utilization supply chain, it has not been proved by actual cases. This paper only
\par
studies a CDW resource utilization supply chain that is composed of one remanufacturer
\par
and one recycler. In reality, there are many remanufacturers and recyclers. In the future, the
\par
researchers may consider recyclers' information sharing strategies in the case of multiple
\par
remanufacturers or multiple recyclers. In addition, this paper only considers the situation
\par
in which the recycler makes demand forecasts and the researchers could go on to study the
\par
situation in which the recycler and the remanufacturer make demand forecasts at the same
\par
time.
